package Datamunge::Expr;

=head1 Datamunge::Expr

A symbolic expression tree over +, -, *, /, ^ (power) and sin/cos/exp/log, supporting
symbolic differentiation, basic algebraic simplification, and numeric evaluation.
Value type wrapping a shared, immutable node tree -- copies are cheap and structural
sharing across copies is always safe since nodes are never mutated after
construction.

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::algebra::Expr>.

Inherits from L<Datamunge::NativeObject|/Datamunge::NativeObject>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::NativeObject');
use overload
    '""' => sub { $_[0]->to_string },
    'bool' => sub { 1 },
    fallback => 1;

=head2 new

    Datamunge::Expr->new($value);

Construct a symbolic constant from a C++ numeric value, including implicit conversion.

Calls the C++ constructor C<datamunge::algebra::Expr>.

Numeric arguments convert to double, matching constant(); integers larger than double's
exact integer range may round. Infinity and NaN are accepted. The new immutable node
owns its value. Enables literals on either side of expression operators and assignment
such as Expr f = 1. Does not add a conversion from Expr back to a number; use evaluate().

B<Example>

    dm::algebra::Expr value = 2.5;
    std::print("{}\n", value.evaluate({}, {})); // 2.5

Overloads (C++ picks one by the arguments' types):

=over 4

=item *

C<Datamunge::Expr-E<gt>new()>

=item *

C<Datamunge::Expr-E<gt>new($value (number))>

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$value> (number, optional)

Numeric constant, stored as a double without evaluation or simplification.

=back

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::bad_alloc>): If the constant node cannot be allocated.

See also: C<constant()>, C<evaluate()>.

=cut

sub new {
    my $class = shift;
    my $self = bless {}, ref($class) || $class;
    my @args = @_;
    Datamunge::Rosetta::construct($self, 285, 0, \@args);
    return $self;
}

=head2 constant

    my $result = Datamunge::Expr->constant($value);

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::algebra::Expr::constant>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$value> (number)

=back

Returns Datamunge::Expr.

=cut

sub constant {
    shift;
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(267, [@_]);
}

