=over 4

=item C<$title_hint> (string, optional)

Window title; empty (the default) uses the plot's title.

=back

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::runtime_error>): If X11 support was not compiled in, the display cannot be opened, its visual is unsupported, or the plot has no data series.

See also: C<save()>, C<to_svg()>.

=cut

sub show {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(543, [@_]);
}

package Datamunge::RPlot;

=head1 Datamunge::RPlot

A single R-base-graphics-style plot, spelled C<dm::Plot> in user code.

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot>.

Covers R's high-level plotting calls (C<plot()>, C<hist()>, C<barplot()>, C<boxplot()>, C<pie()>,
C<curve()>, C<qqnorm()>, ...) as constructors and static factories, plus the "add to the
current plot" verbs R exposes as separate functions (C<points()>, C<lines()>, C<abline()>,
C<legend()>, C<text()>, ...) as chainable member functions. A plot is an ordinary value: it
owns copies of its data, copies cheaply enough to pass around, and draws nothing until it
is rendered with to_svg(), save(), or show().

Two composition styles mix freely. Setters such as set_title() and verbs such as points()
mutate the plot and return C<RPlot&> for chaining; the pipe C<plot | dm::points(...)> (see
compose.hpp) appends a reusable layer to a copy. A fresh plot has the title
"datamunge plot", axis labels "x" and "y", a 960x640 canvas, and a light grid.

B<Example>

    const auto x = dm::seql(-2.0, 2.0, 9);
    auto plt = dm::Plot(x, x * x)
                   .set_title("Parabola")
                   .set_xlab("x")
                   .set_ylab("x squared");
    plt.lines(x, x * x, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}).abline_h(0.0);
    plt.save("parabola.svg");

Inherits from L<Datamunge::Plot|/Datamunge::Plot>.

See also: C<dm::points()>, C<dm::legend()>, C<dm::Histogram>, C<dm::Splom>, L<Datamunge::RLayout|/Datamunge::RLayout>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::Plot');

=head2 new

    Datamunge::RPlot->new($x, $y);

Constructs an empty plot with default labels and styling, ready for layers.

Calls the C++ constructor C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot>.

B<Example>

    auto plt = dm::Plot();
    std::print("{} | {} | {}\n", plt.title_text(), plt.x_label_text(), plt.y_label_text());
    // datamunge plot | x | y

Overloads (C++ picks one by the arguments' types):

=over 4

=item *

C<Datamunge::RPlot-E<gt>new()>

=item *

C<Datamunge::RPlot-E<gt>new($x (array reference of number), $y (array reference of number))>

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$x> (array reference of number, optional)

Horizontal coordinates, one per point.

=item C<$y> (array reference of number, optional)

Vertical coordinates, paired with C<x> by position.

=back

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If the coordinates are empty or have different lengths.

=cut

sub new {
    my $class = shift;
    my $self = bless {}, ref($class) || $class;
    my @args = @_;
    Datamunge::Rosetta::construct($self, 634, 0, \@args);
    return $self;
}

