=head2 set_col

    my $result = $r_plot->set_col($color);

Recolors every data series already on the plot (R's C<col => applied after the fact).

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot::set_col>.

Every existing series (points, lines, bars, ...) takes C<color>, and per-point palettes
from dm::points(...).set_col() or points_colored() are cleared, so the whole plot becomes
one color. Only layers present now are affected: points added later keep their own
colors. Reference lines (abline) and manual legend entries keep their explicit colors.

B<Example>

    auto plt = dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0, 2.0, 3.0), dm::c(3.0, 1.0, 2.0))
                   .set_col(dm::RGB{220, 100, 255});
    std::print("{}\n", plt.series()[0].color.r);  // 220

Overloads (C++ picks one by the arguments' types):

=over 4

=item *

C<$r_plot-E<gt>set_col($color (Datamunge::Rgb)) returning Datamunge::RPlot>

=item *

C<$r_plot-E<gt>set_col($color (string)) returning Datamunge::RPlot>

=item *

C<$r_plot-E<gt>set_col($color (string or undef)) returning Datamunge::RPlot>

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$color> (Datamunge::Rgb | string | string or undef)

New color, including its alpha.

=back

Returns Datamunge::RPlot: This plot, for chaining.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): For unknown names or malformed hex; the plot is unchanged.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): For null, empty or malformed color text; leaves the plot unchanged.

See also: C<set_col(std::string)>, C<dm::points()>.

=cut

sub set_col {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(558, [@_]);
}

