=head2 x_limits

    my $result = $r_plot->x_limits($min_x, $max_x);

Fixes the visible x range, like R's C<xlim = c(min_x, max_x)>.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot::x_limits>.

Without limits the x range is fitted to every layer (and vertical reference line), plus
5% padding on each side. With limits, the plotting area spans exactly [C<min_x>, C<max_x>]
with no padding (R pads C<xlim> by 4% unless C<xaxs = "i">). Data outside the range is not
clipped and may be drawn over the margins.

B<Example>

    auto plt = dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0, 2.0, 3.0), dm::c(1.0, 4.0, 9.0))
                   .x_limits(0.0, 4.0)
                   .y_limits(0.0, 10.0);
    std::print("{} {} {}\n", plt.has_x_limits(), plt.x_max(), plt.y_max());  // true 4 10

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

R C<plot.default()> (C<xlim>, C<ylim>): https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/plot.default.html

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$min_x> (number)

Left edge of the plotting area, in data units.

=item C<$max_x> (number)

Right edge; must be greater than C<min_x>.

=back

Returns Datamunge::RPlot: This plot, for chaining.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Unless C<max_x E<gt> min_x> (NaN fails); the plot is unchanged.

See also: C<y_limits()>.

=cut

