=head2 margins

    my $result = $r_plot->margins($left, $right, $top, $bottom);

Overrides the automatic outer margins around the plotting area, in pixels, like
R's C<par(mai =)> (which uses inches and orders bottom, left, top, right).

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot::margins>.

The automatic margins are left 90, right 24, top 72 (96 when a subtitle is set), and
bottom 84; they leave room for the title, axis labels, and ticks. Explicit margins are used
as given, with no extra room for a subtitle. Tight margins suit small multi-panel tiles
whose title and axis labels are empty. Rendering throws std::runtime_error if the margins
leave no plotting area.

B<Example>

    auto tile = dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0, 2.0), dm::c(2.0, 1.0))
                    .set_title("").set_xlab("").set_ylab("")
                    .margins(30, 10, 10, 20)
                    .tick_font_size(9);
    std::print("{} {}\n", tile.has_custom_margins(), tile.margin_left());  // true 30

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

R C<par()> (C<mai>, C<mar>): https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/par.html

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$left> (number)

Left margin in pixels.

=item C<$right> (number)

Right margin in pixels.

=item C<$top> (number)

Top margin in pixels.

=item C<$bottom> (number)

Bottom margin in pixels.

=back

Returns Datamunge::RPlot: This plot, for chaining.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If any margin is negative or non-finite; the plot is unchanged.

See also: C<size()>, C<tick_font_size()>, L<Datamunge::RLayout|/Datamunge::RLayout>.

=cut

