=head2 pretty_ticks

    my $result = $r_plot->pretty_ticks($enabled);

Places numeric ticks at round values (default on) instead of six evenly spaced
ticks across the range, in the spirit of R's C<pretty()>.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot::pretty_ticks>.

The step is 1, 2, or 5 times a power of ten, chosen as the "nice number" nearest a fifth
of the visible range (Heckbert's rule), and ticks are its multiples inside the range.
That gives about four to seven ticks, close to R's C<pretty()>: a padded range of -0.365
to 7.665 gets ticks at 0, 2, 4 and 6. Custom tick labels take precedence. The background
grid follows the ticks.

B<Example>

    auto plt = dm::Plot(dm::c(0.0, 100.0), dm::c(0.0, 1.0)).pretty_ticks();
    std::print("{}\n", plt.to_svg().contains(">40</text>"));  // true: ticks 0, 20, ..., 100

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

R C<pretty()>: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/base/html/pretty.html

=item *

P. S. Heckbert (1990). "Nice Numbers for Graph Labels". In Graphics Gems, 61-63.
Academic Press. doi:10.1016/B978-0-08-050753-8.50020-8

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$enabled = true>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$enabled> (boolean, optional)

True turns round ticks on; false restores six even ticks.

=back

Returns Datamunge::RPlot: This plot, for chaining.

See also: C<x_ticks()>, C<show_grid()>.

=cut

sub pretty_ticks {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(574, [@_]);
}

=head2 plot

    my $result = Datamunge::RPlot->plot($x, $y, $type, $label, $color);

Builds a scatter, line, or line-plus-points plot of C<y> against C<x>, like R's
C<plot(x, y, type = "p" | "l" | "b")>.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot::plot>.

C<"p"> (the default) adds one scatter series with 4-pixel markers; C<"l"> adds one 2-pixel
line joining the points in input order; C<"b"> adds the line and then unlabeled points on
top (R's C<"b"> leaves gaps in the line around each point; this draws it through them). The
title and axis labels keep their defaults. C<RPlot(x, y)> is C<plot(x, y)>.

B<Example>

    const auto x = dm::seql(0.0, 4.0, 5);
    auto plt = dm::Plot::plot(x, x * x, "b", "x squared");
    std::print("{} series\n", plt.series().size());  // 2 series

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

R C<plot.default()>: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/plot.default.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$type = 'p'>, C<$label = ''>, C<$color = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$x> (array reference of number)

Horizontal coordinates, one per point.

=item C<$y> (array reference of number)

Vertical coordinates, paired with C<x> by position.

=item C<$type> (string, optional)

C<"p"> (default), C<"l">, or C<"b">; case-sensitive.

=item C<$label> (string, optional)

Legend text; empty (the default) keeps the plot out of the automatic legend. With C<"b"> only the line carries it.

=item C<$color> (Datamunge::Rgb, optional)

Color of the points and line (default blue C<{37, 99, 235}>).

=back

Returns Datamunge::RPlot: A new plot holding the series.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If the coordinates are empty or differ in length, or C<type> is not one of the three codes.

See also: C<RPlot(std::vectorE<lt>doubleE<gt>>, C<std::vectorE<lt>doubleE<gt>)>, C<points()>, C<lines()>.

=cut

sub plot {
    shift;
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(575, [@_]);
}

=head2 hist

    my $result = Datamunge::RPlot->hist($data, $bins, $label, $color);

Builds a frequency histogram of C<data> with C<bins> equal-width bins, like R's
C<hist(x, breaks = bins)>.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot::hist>.

The bins split [min, max] of the data into equal widths (R instead treats C<breaks> as a
suggestion and uses C<pretty()> boundaries). Bins include their left edge and exclude their
right edge, except that the maximum goes in the final bin; R's default (C<right = TRUE>) is
the opposite, so a value on an inner boundary is counted one bin higher here. Constant
data uses the range [value, value + 1]. Each bin is one bar of height equal to its count,
centered on the bin with width equal to the bin width. The title is "Histogram", the x
label "x", and the y label "Frequency". The data is consumed; the plot keeps only counts.

B<Example>

    auto plt = dm::Plot::hist({1.0, 2.0, 2.0, 3.0, 3.0, 3.0, 4.0}, 3);
    const auto& bars = plt.series()[0];  // bins [1,2) [2,3) [3,4]
    std::print("{} {} {}\n", bars.y[0], bars.y[1], bars.y[2]);  // 1 2 4

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

R C<hist()>: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/hist.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$bins = 10>, C<$label = ''>, C<$color = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$data> (array reference of number)

Observations; all must be finite.

=item C<$bins> (integer, optional)

Number of bins (default 10).

=item C<$label> (string, optional)

Legend text; empty (the default) shows no legend.

=item C<$color> (Datamunge::Rgb, optional)

Bar color (default light blue C<{96, 165, 250}>).

=back

Returns Datamunge::RPlot: A new plot holding one Bar series.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): For empty/non-finite data, zero bins, or a range/bin width that cannot be represented as a finite positive double.

See also: C<dm::Histogram>, C<bars()>.

=cut

sub hist {
    shift;
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(576, [@_]);
}

=head2 barplot

    my $result = Datamunge::RPlot->barplot($heights, $names, $label, $color);

Builds a bar chart with one bar per height, like R's
C<barplot(heights, names.arg = names)>.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot::barplot>.

Bar i is centered at x = i (0, 1, 2, ...) and is 0.8 units wide (R puts default bars at
0.7, 1.9, 3.1, ...). Bars rise from 0, so negative heights point down, and the y range
always includes 0. Nonempty C<names> become x tick labels under the bars. The title and
axis labels keep their defaults.

B<Example>

    auto plt = dm::Plot::barplot({3.0, 5.0, 2.0}, {"a", "b", "c"}).set_title("Counts");
    std::print("{} {}\n", plt.series()[0].x[2], plt.x_tick_label_list()[2]);  // 2 c

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

R C<barplot()>: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/barplot.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$names = C++ default>, C<$label = ''>, C<$color = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$heights> (array reference of number)

Bar heights, in display order.

=item C<$names> (array reference of string, optional)

Tick labels, one per bar; empty (the default) keeps numeric ticks.

=item C<$label> (string, optional)

Legend text; empty (the default) shows no legend.

=item C<$color> (Datamunge::Rgb, optional)

Bar color (default green C<{22, 163, 74}>).

=back

Returns Datamunge::RPlot: A new plot holding one Bar series.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If C<heights> is empty or C<names> is nonempty with a different length.

See also: C<bars()>, C<x_tick_labels()>.

=cut

sub barplot {
    shift;
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(577, [@_]);
}

=head2 boxplot

    my $result = Datamunge::RPlot->boxplot($groups, $names, $color);

Builds side-by-side box-and-whisker plots, one per group, like R's
C<boxplot(list(...), names = names)>.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot::boxplot>.

Group g is drawn at x = g (0, 1, 2, ...) as a 0.6-unit-wide box from the first to the
third quartile with a line at the median. Quartiles are R's default "type 7" sample
quantiles. Whiskers reach the most extreme values within 1.5 IQR of the box (Tukey's
rule), and values beyond are drawn as outlier points. R's C<boxplot()> instead uses
Tukey's hinges (C<fivenum()>), so for some sample sizes the box edges and the fences differ
slightly from R: for 1, ..., 9, 40 this gives quartiles 3.25 and 7.75 where R draws
hinges at 3 and 8. All boxes share C<color>; the title and axis labels keep their defaults.

B<Example>

    auto plt = dm::Plot::boxplot({{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 40}, {2, 4, 6}}, {"a", "b"});
    const auto& y = plt.series()[0].y;  // whisker_lo, q1, median, q3, whisker_hi, outliers...
    std::print("{} {} {} {} {} | {}\n", y[0], y[1], y[2], y[3], y[4], y[5]);
    // 1 3.25 5.5 7.75 9 | 40
    plt.save("boxes.svg");

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

J. W. Tukey (1977). Exploratory Data Analysis. Addison-Wesley. (Box plot and the 1.5 IQR fences.)

=item *

R. McGill, J. W. Tukey, W. A. Larsen (1978). "Variations of Box Plots". The American
Statistician 32(1), 12-16. doi:10.1080/00031305.1978.10479236

=item *

R. J. Hyndman, Y. Fan (1996). "Sample Quantiles in Statistical Packages". The American
Statistician 50(4), 361-365. doi:10.1080/00031305.1996.10473566 (quantile type 7.)

=item *

R C<boxplot()>: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/boxplot.html

=item *

R C<boxplot.stats()>: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/grDevices/html/boxplot.stats.html

=back

Overloads (C++ picks one by the arguments' types):

=over 4

=item *

C<Datamunge::RPlot-E<gt>boxplot($groups (array reference of array reference of number), $names (array reference of string) = C++ default, $color (Datamunge::Rgb) = C++ default) returning Datamunge::RPlot>

=item *

C<Datamunge::RPlot-E<gt>boxplot($groups (array reference of array reference of number), $names (array reference of string), $colors (array reference of Datamunge::Rgb)) returning Datamunge::RPlot>

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$groups> (array reference of array reference of number)

One vector of observations per box; each must be nonempty.

=item C<$names> (array reference of string, optional)

Tick labels under the boxes; empty (the default) keeps numeric ticks.

=item C<$color> (Datamunge::Rgb | array reference of Datamunge::Rgb, optional)

Box, whisker, and outlier color (default light blue C<{96, 165, 250}>).

=back

Returns Datamunge::RPlot: A new plot holding one Box series per group (see DataSeries::Kind::Box for its layout).

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If C<groups> or any group is empty, or C<names> is nonempty with a different length.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): As boxplot(), or if C<colors> is empty.

See also: C<box()>, C<x_tick_labels()>.

=cut

sub boxplot {
    shift;
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(578, [@_]);
}

=head2 pie

    my $result = Datamunge::RPlot->pie($values, $names, $colors);

Builds a pie chart with one wedge per value, like R's
C<pie(x, labels = names, col = colors, clockwise = TRUE)>.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot::pie>.

Wedge i spans the fraction values[i] / sum(values) of the circle. Wedges start at 12
o'clock and run clockwise (R's default starts at 3 o'clock and runs counter-clockwise).
Each wedge is a filled unit-radius polygon with 24 arc segments. Names, when given, are
drawn at radius 1.15 beside the middle of each wedge's arc and also label the wedges, so the
automatic legend lists them. Colors are recycled; empty uses an 8-color built-in palette
starting blue, red, green. The axes and grid are hidden and both ranges are fixed at
[-1.4, 1.4]. The pie is drawn in data units, so it is round only when the plotting area is
square, e.g. C<.size(598, 640)> with the default margins; the default 960x640 canvas draws an
ellipse.

B<Example>

    auto plt = dm::Plot::pie({3.0, 2.0, 1.0}, {"rent", "food", "fun"}).size(598, 640);
    std::print("{}\n", plt.series().size());  // 6
    plt.save("budget.svg");

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

R C<pie()>: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/pie.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$names = C++ default>, C<$colors = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$values> (array reference of number)

Wedge sizes; nonnegative, with a positive sum.

=item C<$names> (array reference of string, optional)

Wedge labels; empty (the default) draws none.

=item C<$colors> (array reference of Datamunge::Rgb, optional)

Wedge colors, recycled; empty (the default) uses the built-in palette.

=back

Returns Datamunge::RPlot: A new plot holding one Polygon series per wedge plus one Text series per name.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If C<values> is empty, has a negative entry, or sums to 0, or C<names> is nonempty with a different length.

See also: C<polygon()>, C<text()>.

=cut

sub pie {
    shift;
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(579, [@_]);
}

=head2 curve

    my $result = Datamunge::RPlot->curve($f, $from, $to, $n, $label, $color);

Plots a function over [C<from>, C<to>] as a line, like R's C<curve(expr, from, to, n)>.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot::curve>.

Evaluates C<f.call(x)> at C<n> evenly spaced points including both ends, in increasing
order, and joins them with a 2-pixel line. C<f> is used only during this call and is not
retained. Non-finite results are stored as-is. Exceptions thrown by C<f.call()> propagate.

B<Example>

    struct Square : dm::Callback {
      double call(double x) override { return x * x; }
    };
    Square square;
    auto plt = dm::Plot::curve(square, -1.0, 1.0, 5);
    std::print("{}\n", plt.series()[0].y);  // [1, 0.25, 0, 0.25, 1]

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

R C<curve()>: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/curve.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$n = 101>, C<$label = ''>, C<$color = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$f> (Datamunge::Callback)

The function to draw: a dm::Callback subclass overriding C<call(double)>.

=item C<$from> (number)

Left end of the range.

=item C<$to> (number)

Right end; must be greater than C<from>.

=item C<$n> (integer, optional)

Number of sample points, at least 2 (default 101, as in R).

=item C<$label> (string, optional)

Legend text; empty (the default) shows no legend.

=item C<$color> (Datamunge::Rgb, optional)

Line color (default red C<{220, 38, 38}>).

=back

Returns Datamunge::RPlot: A new plot holding one Line series.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If C<n> is below 2 or C<to> is not greater than C<from>.

See also: C<lines()>.

=cut

sub curve {
    shift;
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(580, [@_]);
}

=head2 qqnorm

    my $result = Datamunge::RPlot->qqnorm($data, $label, $color);

Builds a normal Q-Q plot: the sorted C<data> against standard-normal quantiles,
like R's C<qqnorm(y)>.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot::qqnorm>.

The i-th smallest value (i = 1..n) is plotted against the normal quantile of the plotting
position (i - 0.5) / n. R's C<ppoints()> uses that rule only for n E<gt> 10 and
(i - 3/8) / (n + 1/4) for n E<lt>= 10, so the x coordinates differ from R for small samples.
Points that fall on a straight line indicate normally distributed data; add the reference
line with qqline(). The title is "Normal Q-Q", the axis labels "Theoretical Quantiles"
and "Sample Quantiles".

B<Example>

    const std::vector<double> sample{3.0, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0, 4.0};
    auto plt = dm::Plot::qqnorm(sample).qqline(sample);
    std::print("{:.4f}\n", plt.series()[0].x[0]);  // -1.2816

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

M. B. Wilk, R. Gnanadesikan (1968). "Probability plotting methods for the analysis of
data". Biometrika 55(1), 1-17. doi:10.1093/biomet/55.1.1

=item *

R C<qqnorm()>: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/qqnorm.html

=item *

R C<ppoints()>: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/ppoints.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$label = 'sample'>, C<$color = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$data> (array reference of number)

The sample; at least two values. Consumed (sorted in place).

=item C<$label> (string, optional)

Legend text (default "sample", so the automatic legend shows one row; pass "" to hide it).

=item C<$color> (Datamunge::Rgb, optional)

Point color (default blue C<{37, 99, 235}>).

=back

Returns Datamunge::RPlot: A new plot holding one Scatter series.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If C<data> has fewer than two values.

See also: C<qqline()>.

=cut

sub qqnorm {
    shift;
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(581, [@_]);
}

=head2 points

    my $result = $r_plot->points($x, $y, $label, $color, $marker_size);

Adds a scatter series to this plot, like R's C<points(x, y, col = color)>.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot::points>.

The coordinates are copied (or moved from rvalues) into a new series drawn above the
existing ones; axis ranges grow to include it. A nonempty C<label> adds a row to the
automatic legend (shown only while no manual legend() entries exist).

B<Example>

    const auto x = dm::seql(0.0, 6.0, 13);
    auto plt = dm::Plot().set_title("sin and cos");
    plt.points(x, dm::sin(x), "sin", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235})
       .points(x, dm::cos(x), "cos", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 6.0);
    plt.save("trig.svg");  // automatic legend lists "sin" and "cos"

