=head2 qqline

    my $result = $r_plot->qqline($data, $color, $stroke_width);

Adds the reference line of a normal Q-Q plot, like R's C<qqline(y)>: the line
through the first and third quartiles of C<data> and of the standard normal.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot::qqline>.

Pass the same data given to qqnorm(). The sample quartiles are R's default "type 7"
quantiles, as in R's C<qqline()> defaults (C<probs = c(0.25, 0.75)>, C<qtype = 7>). The
slope is the sample IQR divided by the normal IQR (about 1.349), so for normal data it
estimates the standard deviation and the intercept estimates the mean. Added through
abline().

B<Example>

    const std::vector<double> sample{3.0, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0, 4.0};
    auto plt = dm::Plot::qqnorm(sample).qqline(sample);
    const auto& line = plt.reference_lines()[0];
    std::print("{:.4f} {:.4f}\n", line.value, line.slope);  // 3.0000 1.4826

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

R C<qqline()>: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/qqnorm.html

=item *

R. J. Hyndman, Y. Fan (1996). "Sample Quantiles in Statistical Packages". The American
Statistician 50(4), 361-365. doi:10.1080/00031305.1996.10473566

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$color = C++ default>, C<$stroke_width = 1.5>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$data> (array reference of number)

The sample; at least two values. Taken by value, so the caller's data is unchanged.

=item C<$color> (Datamunge::Rgb, optional)

Line color (default red C<{220, 38, 38}>).

=item C<$stroke_width> (number, optional)

Line width in pixels (default 1.5).

=back

Returns Datamunge::RPlot: This plot, for chaining.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If C<data> has fewer than two values.

B<Warning:> Only to_svg() and C<.svg> saves draw reference lines. The raster outputs (C<.png>, C<.pdf>, and show()) currently omit them.

See also: C<qqnorm()>, C<abline()>.

=cut

sub qqline {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(591, [@_]);
}

=head2 legend

    my $result = $r_plot->legend($labels, $colors);

Adds manual legend rows, like R's C<legend(legend = labels, col = colors)>.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot::legend>.

Each label is paired with the color at the same position and drawn as a swatch row in
the legend box. Once any manual row exists, it replaces the automatic legend built from
labeled series. Calling again appends more rows. Unlike dm::legend(), colors are not
recycled here: the vectors must match in length. Empty vectors add nothing.

B<Example>

    const auto up = dm::RGB{37, 99, 235};
    const auto down = dm::RGB{220, 38, 38};
    auto plt = dm::Plot();
    plt.points(dm::c(1.0, 2.0), dm::c(1.0, 2.0), up)
       .points(dm::c(1.0, 2.0), dm::c(2.0, 1.0), down)
       .legend({"rising", "falling"}, {up, down});
    std::print("{}\n", plt.legend_entries().size());  // 2

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

R C<legend()>: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/legend.html

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$labels> (array reference of string)

Row texts, in display order.

=item C<$colors> (array reference of Datamunge::Rgb)

Swatch colors, one per label.

=back

Returns Datamunge::RPlot: This plot, for chaining.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If C<labels> and C<colors> differ in length.

See also: C<dm::legend() for the recycling>, C<pipe-friendly layer.>.

=cut

sub legend {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(592, [@_]);
}

=head2 text

    my $result = $r_plot->text($x, $y, $label, $color, $font_size);

Draws C<label> centered on the data point (C<x>, C<y>), like R's
C<text(x, y, labels = label)>.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot::text>.

The text is centered both horizontally and vertically on the point, like R's default
C<adj>. The point counts toward the automatic axis ranges, but the text's extent does not,
so long labels near the edge can overflow. Text layers never appear in the legend, and
set_col() recolors them along with the data.

B<Example>

    auto plt = dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0, 2.0, 3.0), dm::c(1.0, 4.0, 2.0))
                   .text(2.0, 4.4, "peak", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 16.0);
    std::print("{} {}\n", plt.series()[1].label, plt.series()[1].marker_size);  // peak 16

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

R C<text()>: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/text.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$color = C++ default>, C<$font_size = 14.0>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$x> (number)

Horizontal position, in data units.

=item C<$y> (number)

Vertical position, in data units.

=item C<$label> (string)

The text to draw (XML-escaped in SVG output).

=item C<$color> (Datamunge::Rgb, optional)

Text color (default near-black C<{17, 24, 39}>).

=item C<$font_size> (number, optional)

Font size in pixels (default 14).

=back

Returns Datamunge::RPlot: This plot, for chaining.

See also: C<pie()>, C<legend()>.

=cut

sub text {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(593, [@_]);
}

=head2 polygon

    my $result = $r_plot->polygon($x, $y, $color, $filled);

Draws the closed polygon through the vertices (x[i], y[i]), like R's
C<polygon(x, y, col = color)>.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot::polygon>.

The last vertex joins the first automatically. Filled polygons (the default) are painted
with C<color> (plus a 1-pixel outline in SVG; the raster fill uses the even-odd rule);
unfilled ones draw a 2-pixel outline only (R's default is unfilled). Translucent colors work well
for shaded regions such as confidence bands. Vertices count toward the axis ranges.

B<Example>

    auto plt = dm::Plot()
                   .polygon({0.0, 1.0, 0.5}, {0.0, 0.0, 1.0}, dm::RGBA{37, 99, 235, 0.4})
                   .polygon({0.2, 0.8, 0.8, 0.2}, {0.1, 0.1, 0.4, 0.4}, dm::RGB{0, 0, 0}, false);
    std::print("{}\n", plt.series()[1].filled);  // false

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

R C<polygon()>: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/polygon.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$color = C++ default>, C<$filled = true>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$x> (array reference of number)

Vertex x coordinates, in order around the polygon.

=item C<$y> (array reference of number)

Vertex y coordinates, paired with C<x>.

=item C<$color> (Datamunge::Rgb, optional)

Fill (and outline) color (default blue C<{37, 99, 235}>).

=item C<$filled> (boolean, optional)

True (the default) fills; false draws the outline only.

=back

Returns Datamunge::RPlot: This plot, for chaining.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If the coordinates are empty or have different lengths.

See also: C<pie()>, C<segments()>.

=cut

sub polygon {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(594, [@_]);
}

=head2 image

    my $result = $r_plot->image($x, $y, $z, $palette, $z_min, $z_max);

Draws a grid of coloured rectangles, one per value of C<z>, like R's C<image(x, y, z)>.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot::image>.

Cell (i, j) is centred on (x[i], y[j]) and reaches halfway to its neighbours (a half step
beyond the outermost centres). Its colour is the palette entry for z[i][j] on a linear
scale from C<z_min> to C<z_max>; NaN cells are left empty. Each cell is added as a filled
polygon, so the grid should have at most tens of thousands of cells.

B<Example>

    auto plt = dm::graphics::RPlot::create().image({0.0, 1.0}, {0.0, 1.0, 2.0}, {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}});
    std::print("{}\n", plt.series().size());  // 6

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

R C<image()>: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/image.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$palette = C++ default>, C<$z_min = C++ default>, C<$z_max = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$x> (array reference of number)

Cell centres along x, increasing (at least 2).

=item C<$y> (array reference of number)

Cell centres along y, increasing (at least 2).

=item C<$z> (array reference of array reference of number)

Values, z[i][j] at (x[i], y[j]); z.size() == x.size() and each row has y.size() entries.

=item C<$palette> (array reference of Datamunge::Rgb, optional)

Colours from low to high (default terrain_colors(12)).

=item C<$z_min> (number, optional)

Value mapped to the first colour; NaN (the default) uses the smallest z.

=item C<$z_max> (number, optional)

Value mapped to the last colour; NaN (the default) uses the largest z.

=back

Returns Datamunge::RPlot: This plot, for chaining.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If the sizes do not match, x or y is not increasing, or the palette is empty.

See also: C<terrain_colors()>, C<polygon()>.

=cut

sub image {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(595, [@_]);
}

=head2 segments

    my $result = $r_plot->segments($x0, $y0, $x1, $y1, $color, $stroke_width);

Draws one straight segment from (x0[i], y0[i]) to (x1[i], y1[i]) for each i, like
R's C<segments(x0, y0, x1, y1)>.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RPlot::segments>.

All segments share C<color> and C<stroke_width> and form one Segment layer; their end
points count toward the axis ranges. Useful for error bars, residual sticks, and
dendrograms.

B<Example>

    const auto x = dm::c(1.0, 2.0, 3.0);
    const auto y = dm::c(2.0, 3.5, 3.0);
    auto plt = dm::Plot(x, y).segments(x, y - 0.5, x, y + 0.5);  // +/- 0.5 error bars
    std::print("{}\n", plt.series()[1].x.size());  // 6 (start, end, start, end, ...)

