package Datamunge::RLayout;

=head1 Datamunge::RLayout

Tiles independently built plots into one multi-panel figure, like R's
C<par(mfrow = c(rows, cols))> followed by one plot per panel.

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::graphics::RLayout>.

Create it with create(rows, cols), then add() up to rows * cols panels; they fill the grid
row by row. Each panel is copied (and its SVG rendered) at add() time, so later changes to
the original plot do not show and panels need not outlive the layout. Each cell is
C<width / cols> by C<(height - band) / rows> pixels, where band is title_band_px when a
figure title is set and 0 otherwise; every panel is scaled from its own canvas size into its
cell. The figure background is white. Unused cells stay empty.

B<Example>

    const auto x = dm::seql(0.0, 6.0, 30);
    auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2)
                   .size(1200, 500)
                   .title("Two views")
                   .add(dm::Plot(x, dm::sin(x)).set_title("sin"))
                   .add(dm::Plot(x, dm::cos(x)).set_title("cos"));
    std::print("{} panels in {}x{}\n", fig.panel_count(), fig.rows(), fig.cols());
    // 2 panels in 1x2
    fig.save("two_views.svg");

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

R C<par()> (C<mfrow>): https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/par.html

=back

Inherits from L<Datamunge::NativeObject|/Datamunge::NativeObject>.

See also: L<Datamunge::RPlot|/Datamunge::RPlot>, C<RPlot::margins()>, C<dm::Splom>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::NativeObject');

=head2 new

    Datamunge::RLayout->new();

Calls the C++ constructor C<datamunge::graphics::RLayout>.

=cut

sub new {
    my $class = shift;
    my $self = bless {}, ref($class) || $class;
    my @args = @_;
    Datamunge::Rosetta::construct($self, 659, 0, \@args);
    return $self;
}

=head2 create

    my $result = Datamunge::RLayout->create($rows, $cols);

Creates an empty layout with C<rows> by C<cols> cells, like R's
C<par(mfrow = c(rows, cols))>.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RLayout::create>.

The figure starts at 960 by 640 pixels with no title.

B<Example>

See RLayout.

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

R C<par()> (C<mfrow>): https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/par.html

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$rows> (integer)

Number of panel rows; must be positive.

=item C<$cols> (integer)

Number of panel columns; must be positive.

=back

Returns Datamunge::RLayout: The empty layout.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If C<rows> or C<cols> is 0.

See also: C<add()>.

=cut

sub create {
    shift;
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(635, [@_]);
}

=head2 add

    my $result = $r_layout->add($panel);

Copies C<panel> into the next free cell, filling rows left to right, top to bottom.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RLayout::add>.

The panel's SVG is rendered immediately and stored with the copy, so rendering errors
surface here and later edits to the original plot have no effect on the layout.

B<Example>

    auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 1).add(dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0, 2.0), dm::c(2.0, 1.0)));
    try {
      fig.add(dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0), dm::c(1.0)));
    } catch (const std::invalid_argument& e) {
      std::print("{}\n", e.what());  // RLayout::add: layout is already full
    }

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$panel> (Datamunge::Plot)

The plot to add; any RPlot (or other Plot) converts.

=back

Returns Datamunge::RLayout: This layout, for chaining.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If all rows * cols cells are already filled.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::runtime_error>): If C<panel> has no data series or its canvas is smaller than its margins.

See also: C<panel()>, C<panel_count()>.

=cut

sub add {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(636, [@_]);
}

=head2 size

    my $result = $r_layout->size($width, $height);

Sets the size of the whole figure in pixels (default 960 by 640).

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RLayout::size>.

Panels keep their own canvas sizes and are scaled into their cells, so text in a panel
shrinks or grows with it; give panels a size close to their cell for readable labels.

B<Example>

See RLayout.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$width> (integer)

Figure width in pixels.

=item C<$height> (integer)

Figure height in pixels, including the title band.

=back

Returns Datamunge::RLayout: This layout, for chaining.

See also: C<title()>.

=cut

sub size {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(637, [@_]);
}

=head2 dpi

    my $result = $r_layout->dpi($value);

Sets the resolution PNG and PDF output is rasterized at, like R's
C<png(res = )> and ggsave()'s C<dpi => (default 96).

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::graphics::RLayout::dpi>.

The layout, text and line widths keep their size relative to the layout; only the pixel
count changes: a layout of width(units::inch{8}) saves as a 768-pixel-wide PNG at 96 dpi
and a 2400-pixel-wide one at 300 dpi. A PNG records the resolution (its pHYs chunk) so
other programs show it at its physical size; a PDF keeps its page size and gains detail.
SVG output is vector and is unaffected.

B<Example>

    auto grid = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).width(dm::units::inch{6}).dpi(300);
    std::print("{} {}\n", grid.width(), grid.dpi());   // 576 300

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

R png() C<res> argument: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/grDevices/html/png.html

=item *

ggplot2 ggsave() C<dpi> argument: https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/ggsave.html

=back

Overloads (C++ picks one by the arguments' types):

=over 4

=item *

C<$r_layout-E<gt>dpi($value (number)) returning Datamunge::RLayout>

=item *

C<$r_layout-E<gt>dpi() returning number>

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$value> (number, optional)

Dots per inch; finite and positive.

=back

Returns Datamunge::RLayout | number: This layout, for chaining.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If C<value> is not finite and positive.

See also: C<width(units::Length)>, C<height(units::Length)>, C<save()>.

=cut

