package Datamunge::Fem1D;

=head1 Datamunge::Fem1D

Solve steady or transient one-dimensional divergence-form problems with P1 Galerkin FEM.
Models -(p*u')'+q*u=f and u_t-(p*u')'+q*u=f on a caller-supplied mesh.
Requires at least two finite strictly increasing nodes, finite coefficient/
boundary/source data and finite assembled arithmetic with nonzero Thomas pivots;
those mathematical/geometry preconditions are not fully validated. Positive p,
nonnegative q/Robin data and adequate constraints give the usual coercive model.
Piecewise-constant p/q can be scalar or one per element. Results own their
values; input mesh/data are unchanged. Calls share no mutable global state,
while callback side effects/concurrency belong to the caller.

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::fem::FEM1D>.

B<Example>

    const auto r=dm::fem::FEM1D::solve_builtin(dm::fem::make_uniform_mesh1d(0,1,2),{1},{0},"constant",{1},{},{});
    std::print("{}\n",r.value_at(1)); // 0.125

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

MFEM, Weak Formulations: https://mfem.org/fem_weak_form/ . This native implementation
uses P1 intervals, three-point Gauss-Legendre loads and a tridiagonal direct solve.

=back

Inherits from L<Datamunge::NativeObject|/Datamunge::NativeObject>.

See also: L<Datamunge::Mesh1D|/Datamunge::Mesh1D>, L<Datamunge::Fem1DResult|/Datamunge::Fem1DResult>, L<Datamunge::Fem1DTimeSeries|/Datamunge::Fem1DTimeSeries>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::NativeObject');

=head2 new

    Datamunge::Fem1D->new();

Calls the C++ constructor C<datamunge::fem::FEM1D>.

=cut

sub new {
    my $class = shift;
    my $self = bless {}, ref($class) || $class;
    my @args = @_;
    Datamunge::Rosetta::construct($self, 921, 0, \@args);
    return $self;
}

=head2 solve

    my $result = Datamunge::Fem1D->solve($mesh, $p, $q, $f, $left, $right);

Solve the steady P1 system for -(p*u')'+q*u=f with endpoint boundary data.
Follows FEM1D's mesh/coefficient/finite-arithmetic/nonsingular-pivot preconditions.
Uses three-point Gauss-Legendre quadrature of f times each local basis, natural
boundary terms and symmetric Dirichlet elimination, then Thomas elimination.
O(N) work/storage for N nodes; invokes the source three times per element.
No callback pointer is retained. Independent calls share no mutable state.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::fem::FEM1D::solve>.

B<Example>

    struct Unit final:dm::fem::ScalarField1D{double evaluate(double) override{return 1;}} source;
    const auto r=dm::fem::FEM1D::solve(dm::fem::make_uniform_mesh1d(0,1,2),{1},{0},source,{},{});
    std::print("{}\n",r.value_at(1)); // 0.125

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://mfem.org/fem_weak_form/ ; native interval P1 and quadrature choices as FEM1D.

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$mesh> (Datamunge::Mesh1D)

Borrowed mesh with at least two finite strictly increasing nodes.

=item C<$p> (array reference of number)

Borrowed diffusion coefficient: length1 or one per element.

=item C<$q> (array reference of number)

Borrowed reaction coefficient: length1 or one per element.

=item C<$f> (Datamunge::ScalarField1D)

Borrowed spatial source; evaluate() may have caller-owned side effects.

=item C<$left> (Datamunge::BoundaryCondition1D)

Left endpoint condition, with outward normal -1.

=item C<$right> (Datamunge::BoundaryCondition1D)

Right endpoint condition, with outward normal +1.

=back

Returns Datamunge::Fem1DResult: Owned original nodes and computed nodal values.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If p/q are empty or have the wrong lengths.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::bad_alloc>): If assembly/result storage cannot be allocated; callback exceptions propagate.

See also: C<solve_builtin()>, C<solve_transient()>.

=cut

sub solve {
    shift;
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(917, [@_]);
}

=head2 solve_builtin

    my $result = Datamunge::Fem1D->solve_builtin($mesh, $p, $q, $source, $source_params, $left, $right);

Solve the steady system using an owned named spatial source, without a director callback.
Shares the live-source solver and its mathematical/precondition/storage contracts.
Sources: zero=0 (params unused); constant=c ({1}); linear=m*x+c ({1,0});
polynomial=sum(params[i]*x^i) (empty gives0); sine=A*sin(w*x+phase) ({1,1,0}).
Missing trailing parameters use those defaults; extra parameters are ignored
except polynomial, which uses all entries. Name matching is exact/case-sensitive.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::fem::FEM1D::solve_builtin>.

B<Example>

    const auto r=dm::fem::FEM1D::solve_builtin(dm::fem::make_uniform_mesh1d(0,1,2),{1},{0},"constant",{}, {},{});
    std::print("{}\n",r.value_at(1)); // 0.125

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://mfem.org/fem_weak_form/ ; source definitions are native convenience choices.

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$mesh> (Datamunge::Mesh1D)

Borrowed valid finite interval mesh; unchanged.

=item C<$p> (array reference of number)

Borrowed scalar/per-element diffusion coefficient.

=item C<$q> (array reference of number)

Borrowed scalar/per-element reaction coefficient.

=item C<$source> (string)

Exact name zero, constant, linear, polynomial or sine.

=item C<$source_params> (array reference of number)

Borrowed positional source parameters, with defaults described above.

=item C<$left> (Datamunge::BoundaryCondition1D)

Left endpoint data using outward normal -1.

=item C<$right> (Datamunge::BoundaryCondition1D)

Right endpoint data using outward normal +1.

=back

Returns Datamunge::Fem1DResult: Owned computed nodal result, retaining no source or input reference.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): For an unknown source or malformed p/q lengths.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::bad_alloc>): If owned parameter/assembly/result storage cannot be allocated.

See also: C<solve()>, C<solve_transient_builtin()>.

=cut

sub solve_builtin {
    shift;
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(918, [@_]);
}

=head2 solve_transient

    my $result = Datamunge::Fem1D->solve_transient($mesh, $p, $q, $f, $left, $right, $u0, $t_end, $dt);

Integrate u_t-(p*u')'+q*u=f with P1 space and actual backward-Euler time intervals.
Shares FEM1D's spatial/finite-arithmetic/nonzero-pivot preconditions. Source
and boundary data represent a time-independent model; the live callback is
actually reevaluated three times per element at every time advance, not cached.
Stores initial u0 as supplied, then imposes boundary data at later solves.
Nominal step dt is shortened at t_end; actual positive time differences enter
the mass term. Stops once t_end is reached, avoiding a duplicate rounded last
time even when ceil(t_end/dt) overestimates the number of advances.
O(S*N) work/output storage for S advances/N nodes, O(N) assembly temporaries;
no optional trace or quota. Backward Euler is unconditionally stable for the
coercive semidiscrete model, not a guarantee of arbitrary-step accuracy or
finite arithmetic for arbitrary parameters. Input arrays remain unchanged;
callback exceptions/side effects/concurrent safety belong to caller.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::fem::FEM1D::solve_transient>.

B<Example>

    struct Zero final:dm::fem::ScalarField1D{double evaluate(double) override{return 0;}} source;
    const auto t=dm::fem::FEM1D::solve_transient(dm::fem::make_uniform_mesh1d(0,1,2),{1},{0},source,{},{},{0,1,0},.5,.5);
    std::print("{} {:.6f}\n",t.num_steps(),t.value_at(1,1)); // 2 0.142857

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

Arnold (2012), Numerical Analysis of Partial Differential Equations, chapter5 section2.
https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~arnold/8445.f12/notes.pdf . Native load quadrature,
fixed data and rounded/shortened time grid are implementation choices.

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$mesh> (Datamunge::Mesh1D)

Borrowed valid mesh with at least two finite increasing nodes.

=item C<$p> (array reference of number)

Borrowed scalar/per-element diffusion coefficient.

=item C<$q> (array reference of number)

Borrowed scalar/per-element reaction coefficient.

=item C<$f> (Datamunge::ScalarField1D)

Borrowed spatial callback, reevaluated at every advance.

=item C<$left> (Datamunge::BoundaryCondition1D)

Fixed left condition, with outward normal -1.

=item C<$right> (Datamunge::BoundaryCondition1D)

Fixed right condition, with outward normal +1.

=item C<$u0> (array reference of number)

Borrowed finite initial nodal values, exactly one per mesh node.

=item C<$t_end> (number)

Positive finite final time, in time units.

=item C<$dt> (number)

Positive finite nominal time step in the same units; may exceed t_end.

=back

Returns Datamunge::Fem1DTimeSeries: Owned nodes, initial state and strictly advancing slices ending at t_end.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): For fewer than two nodes, wrong coefficient/u0 lengths or nonpositive/nonfinite time controls.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Length> (C++ C<std::length_error>): If frame counts, vector capacities or total scalar count cannot be represented.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Arithmetic> (C++ C<std::overflow_error>): If rounded time products cannot advance before t_end.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::bad_alloc>): If output/assembly storage cannot be allocated; callback exceptions propagate.

See also: C<solve_transient_builtin()>, L<Datamunge::Fem1DTimeSeries|/Datamunge::Fem1DTimeSeries>.

=cut

sub solve_transient {
    shift;
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(919, [@_]);
}

=head2 solve_transient_builtin

    my $result = Datamunge::Fem1D->solve_transient_builtin($mesh, $p, $q, $source, $source_params, $left, $right, $u0, $t_end, $dt);

Integrate the same backward-Euler model with a named source and no callback subclass.
Uses the same private source construction as solve_builtin() and the same
actual time loop as solve_transient(); no second solver or per-language glue.
Names/defaults: zero=0; constant=c ({1}); linear=m*x+c ({1,0});
polynomial=sum(params[i]*x^i) (empty0); sine=A*sin(w*x+phase) ({1,1,0}).
Extra entries ignored except polynomial; exact case-sensitive names. Source
objects are local and synchronous; results own values, not callback aliases.
All spatial/time/initial-state, complexity and stability contracts follow
solve_transient(); ordinary output has no optional history/quota.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::fem::FEM1D::solve_transient_builtin>.

B<Example>

    const auto t=dm::fem::FEM1D::solve_transient_builtin(dm::fem::make_uniform_mesh1d(0,1,2),{1},{0},"constant",{1},{},{},{0,0,0},.5,.5);
    std::print("{} {:.6f}\n",t.num_steps(),t.value_at(1,1)); // 2 0.107143

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~arnold/8445.f12/notes.pdf ; same native discretization as solve_transient().

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$mesh> (Datamunge::Mesh1D)

Borrowed valid finite increasing mesh, with at least two nodes.

=item C<$p> (array reference of number)

Borrowed scalar/per-element diffusion coefficient.

=item C<$q> (array reference of number)

Borrowed scalar/per-element reaction coefficient.

=item C<$source> (string)

Exact name zero, constant, linear, polynomial or sine.

=item C<$source_params> (array reference of number)

Borrowed positional parameters; omitted trailing values use the defaults above.

=item C<$left> (Datamunge::BoundaryCondition1D)

Fixed left boundary condition using outward normal -1.

=item C<$right> (Datamunge::BoundaryCondition1D)

Fixed right boundary condition using outward normal +1.

=item C<$u0> (array reference of number)

Borrowed finite initial values, exactly one per spatial node.

=item C<$t_end> (number)

Positive finite final time.

=item C<$dt> (number)

Positive finite nominal time step; final interval is shortened as needed.

=back

Returns Datamunge::Fem1DTimeSeries: Owned initial state and actual strictly advancing backward-Euler slices.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): For an unknown source, malformed coefficient/u0 lengths, fewer than two nodes or nonpositive/nonfinite time controls.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Length> (C++ C<std::length_error>): If frame/container/total scalar counts cannot be represented.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Arithmetic> (C++ C<std::overflow_error>): If rounded time products cannot advance.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::bad_alloc>): If parameters, output or assembly storage cannot be allocated.

See also: C<solve_builtin()>, C<solve_transient()>.

=cut

sub solve_transient_builtin {
    shift;
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(920, [@_]);
}

