package Datamunge::AlphaBetaFilter;

=head1 Datamunge::AlphaBetaFilter

The constant-gain alpha-beta ("g-h") filter: a constant-velocity tracker that uses
the SAME predict/correct structure as KalmanFilter, but with fixed gains alpha
(position correction) and beta (velocity correction) instead of a covariance-driven
Kalman gain -- it is exactly the steady-state behavior a 1D constant-velocity
KalmanFilter converges to once its covariance stops changing, with the covariance
bookkeeping stripped out entirely. Far cheaper per step (no matrices at all) at the
cost of not adapting its gain to changing noise conditions the way a real Kalman
filter does.

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::filter::AlphaBetaFilter>.

Inherits from L<Datamunge::NativeObject|/Datamunge::NativeObject>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::NativeObject');

=head2 new

    Datamunge::AlphaBetaFilter->new($alpha, $beta, $dt, $x0, $v0);

Calls the C++ constructor C<datamunge::filter::AlphaBetaFilter>.

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$x0 = 0.0>, C<$v0 = 0.0>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$alpha> (number)

=item C<$beta> (number)

=item C<$dt> (number)

=item C<$x0> (number, optional)

=item C<$v0> (number, optional)

=back

=cut

sub new {
    my $class = shift;
    my $self = bless {}, ref($class) || $class;
    my @args = @_;
    Datamunge::Rosetta::construct($self, 1022, 0, \@args);
    return $self;
}

=head2 update

    $alpha_beta_filter->update($z);

Predicts one step ahead, then corrects toward measurement C<z>: x_pred = x +
v*dt; residual = z - x_pred; x = x_pred + alpha*residual; v += (beta/dt)*residual.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::filter::AlphaBetaFilter::update>.

