package Datamunge::KalmanFilter;

=head1 Datamunge::KalmanFilter

Owned fixed-model linear covariance Kalman filter with optional full RTS smoothing.
Models x_k=F*x_(k-1)+w and z_k=H*x_k+v with caller-supplied Q/R covariance.
Inputs are copied; predict/update mutate this instance, independent copies
evolve separately. Concurrent mutation requires synchronization. Shape checks
do not establish finite values, covariance PSD, noise independence or valid
probability assumptions: those remain caller preconditions. Uses explicit LU
inverses and simplified covariance updates, not a Joseph/square-root method.
Under a valid linear Gaussian model the mean is conditional MMSE; floating
output is approximate. No universal realized-error or numerical PSD guarantee.

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::filter::KalmanFilter>.

B<Example>

    dm::filter::KalmanFilter k({{1}},{{1}},{{1}},{{3}},{0},{{4}});
    k.predict();k.update({2});std::print("{}\n",k.state()[0]); // 1.25

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

Kalman(1960), A New Approach to Linear Filtering and Prediction Problems,
https://doi.org/10.1115/1.3662552.

=item *

Rauch,Tung,Striebel(1965), Maximum likelihood estimates of linear dynamic
systems, https://doi.org/10.2514/3.3166.

=back

Inherits from L<Datamunge::NativeObject|/Datamunge::NativeObject>.

See also: C<filter()>, C<smooth()>, L<Datamunge::KalmanState|/Datamunge::KalmanState>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::NativeObject');

=head2 new

    Datamunge::KalmanFilter->new($f, $h, $q, $r, $x0, $p0);

Copy a fixed linear model and initial state, validating matrix dimensions.
No finite/covariance-definiteness checks are added; the caller supplies
statistically valid finite matrices and invertible required innovation/prediction covariances.
O(n*n+m*n+m*m) copied model/state storage for n state and m observation dimensions.

Calls the C++ constructor C<datamunge::filter::KalmanFilter>.

B<Example>

    const dm::filter::KalmanFilter k({{1}},{{1}},{{1}},{{1}},{0},{{2}});
    std::print("{}\n",k.covariance()[0][0]); // 2

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$f> (array reference of array reference of number)

Owned n-by-n transition matrix.

=item C<$h> (array reference of array reference of number)

Owned m-by-n observation matrix.

=item C<$q> (array reference of array reference of number)

Owned n-by-n process covariance.

=item C<$r> (array reference of array reference of number)

Owned m-by-m observation covariance.

=item C<$x0> (array reference of number)

Owned initial length-n state mean.

=item C<$p0> (array reference of array reference of number)

Owned initial n-by-n state covariance.

=back

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): On ragged/incompatible matrix shapes.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::bad_alloc>): On model/state allocation failure.

=cut

sub new {
    my $class = shift;
    my $self = bless {}, ref($class) || $class;
    my @args = @_;
    Datamunge::Rosetta::construct($self, 1056, 0, \@args);
    return $self;
}

=head2 predict

    $kalman_filter->predict();

Predict x=F*x and P=F*P*F^T+Q once, mutating the current state.
Matrix arithmetic is ordinary floating point; nonfinite results propagate.
O(n^3) dense work/O(n^2) temporaries; no rollback guarantee on exceptions.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::filter::KalmanFilter::predict>.

B<Example>

    dm::filter::KalmanFilter k({{1}},{{1}},{{1}},{{1}},{2},{{4}});k.predict();
    std::print("{}\n",k.covariance()[0][0]); // 5

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::bad_alloc>): On temporary allocation failure.

See also: C<update()>.

=cut

sub predict {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(1050, [@_]);
}

=head2 update

    $kalman_filter->update($z);

Correct from z using innovation covariance, LU gain and simplified posterior covariance.
Computes y=z-H*x,S=H*P*H^T+R,K=P*H^T*S^-1, then x+=K*y and
P=(I-K*H)*P. Requires finite statistically valid inputs/invertible S;
shape validation alone does not ensure those properties. Rounding may
lose symmetry/PSD; not Joseph form. Mutates state without rollback guarantee.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::filter::KalmanFilter::update>.

B<Example>

    dm::filter::KalmanFilter k({{1}},{{1}},{{1}},{{3}},{0},{{5}});k.update({2});
    std::print("{}\n",k.state()[0]); // 1.25

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$z> (array reference of number)

Borrowed length-m observation, unchanged by this call.

=back

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): On observation dimension mismatch.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::runtime_error>): On LU failure under its implementation contract.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::bad_alloc>): On temporary allocation failure.

See also: C<predict()>.

=cut

sub update {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(1051, [@_]);
}

=head2 state

    my $result = $kalman_filter->state();

Copy the current state mean without another filter update.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::filter::KalmanFilter::state>.

Returns array reference of number: Owned length-n current mean.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::bad_alloc>): On copy allocation failure.

=cut

sub state {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(1052, [@_]);
}

=head2 covariance

    my $result = $kalman_filter->covariance();

Copy the current covariance without recomputing a filter update.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::filter::KalmanFilter::covariance>.

Returns array reference of array reference of number: Owned n-by-n current covariance.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::bad_alloc>): On copy allocation failure.

=cut

sub covariance {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(1053, [@_]);
}

=head2 filter

    my $result = $kalman_filter->filter($measurements);

Predict then correct every observation in order, owning actual posterior states.
Empty input returns empty and leaves state unchanged. Ends at the final
posterior, so repeated calls continue this instance rather than restarting.
O(T) dense filter steps plus O(T*n^2) owned output; input remains unchanged.
A later invalid measurement can follow already committed predictions/updates;
failure does not roll the whole sequence back.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::filter::KalmanFilter::filter>.

B<Example>

    dm::filter::KalmanFilter k({{1}},{{1}},{{1}},{{1}},{0},{{1}});
    std::print("{}\n",k.filter({{1},{2}}).size()); // 2

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$measurements> (array reference of array reference of number)

Borrowed ordered length-m observations.

=back

Returns array reference of Datamunge::KalmanState: Owned actual causal mean/covariance at every completed step.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): On a measurement dimension mismatch.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::runtime_error>): On underlying LU failure.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::bad_alloc>): On temporary/output allocation failure.

See also: C<smooth()>.

=cut

sub filter {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(1054, [@_]);
}

=head2 smooth

    my $result = $kalman_filter->smooth($measurements);

Execute one actual forward pass and an RTS backward pass on the full sequence.
Owns actual prediction/posterior arrays during execution, then returns
causal and smoothed means/covariances. No repeated-prefix runs. Leaves this
filter at its final forward posterior. Empty input returns empty unchanged.
Exact-model conditional covariance reduces; realized error need not decrease
on every sequence. Ordinary matrix arithmetic can lose symmetry/PSD.
O(T) dense steps/backward solves, O(T*n^2) intermediate/output storage.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::filter::KalmanFilter::smooth>.

B<Example>

    dm::filter::KalmanFilter k({{1}},{{1}},{{1}},{{1}},{0},{{1}});
    const auto r=k.smooth({{1},{2}});std::print("{}\n",r.filtered.back().x==r.smoothed.back().x); // true

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

Rauch,Tung,Striebel(1965), https://doi.org/10.2514/3.3166.

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$measurements> (array reference of array reference of number)

Borrowed full ordered length-m sequence.

=back

Returns Datamunge::SmoothResult: Owned actual forward and full-sequence smoothed trajectories.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): On measurement dimension mismatch.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::runtime_error>): On underlying innovation/prediction LU failure.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::bad_alloc>): On intermediate/output allocation failure.

See also: C<filter()>, L<Datamunge::SmoothResult|/Datamunge::SmoothResult>.

=cut

sub smooth {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(1055, [@_]);
}

