    return;
}

=head2 cells

    my $value = $voronoi_diagram->cells;
    $voronoi_diagram->cells($value);

cells[i] is points[i]'s Voronoi cell boundary, as circumcenters in angular order around points[i]. For an INTERIOR point this is a closed polygon (as with any polygon in this module, the closing edge back to cells[i].front() is implicit). For a point on the convex hull, the true Voronoi cell is unbounded -- this only returns its FINITE portion (an open polyline, missing the two rays to infinity), since robustly constructing those rays is out of scope here. Empty for a point that ended up in no Delaunay triangle (possible for an exactly-duplicated input point). (array reference of array reference of Datamunge::Point2D).

=cut

sub cells {
    my $self = shift;
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(1381, [$self]) unless @_;
    Datamunge::Rosetta::call(1382, [$self, $_[0]]);
    return;
}

package Datamunge::LinalgMatrix;

=head1 Datamunge::LinalgMatrix

Binding counterpart of Mat/SpMat with shared, immutable expression operands.
Transpose, multiplication and inverse actions defer native computation until eval(),
values() or text(). Handles keep temporary operands alive across garbage collection.
Sparse products use CSR; solves use the canonical dense LU/QR implementation.
Concurrent const evaluation is safe; no evaluation cache is mutated.

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::LinalgMatrix>.

B<Example>

    dm::LinalgMatrix A({2, 0, 0, 4}, 2, 2), b({6, 8}, 2, 1);
    auto x = A.inverse_action().multiply(b);
    std::print("{:.0f}\n", x.values()[0]); // 3

Inherits from L<Datamunge::NativeObject|/Datamunge::NativeObject>.

See also: C<Mat>, C<SpMat>, C<solve()>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::NativeObject');

=head2 new

    Datamunge::LinalgMatrix->new($values, $rows, $cols, $sparse);

Construct an owned dense or CSR matrix from row-major values.

Calls the C++ constructor C<datamunge::LinalgMatrix>.

B<Example>

    dm::LinalgMatrix A({1, 0}, 1, 2, true);
    std::print("{}\n", A.cols()); // 2

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$sparse = false>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$values> (array reference of number)

Exactly rows * cols coefficients; sparse zero entries are omitted.

=item C<$rows> (integer)

Number of rows, including zero.

=item C<$cols> (integer)

Number of columns, including zero.

=item C<$sparse> (boolean, optional)

Select CSR storage, default false.

=back

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If dimensions overflow or do not match values.

=cut

