package Datamunge::Rhs;

=head1 Datamunge::Rhs

Caller-defined derivative callback dy/dt=f(t,y), without retained solver-owned references.
The solver requires finite matching-dimensional returns and guards stages/call
budgets before invoking it. User callback exceptions/effects propagate and are
not rolled back. Thread safety belongs to the callback implementation.
Configured SWIG director support is backend-specific; named builtin systems
remain available independently of subclassing support. No all-binding claim.

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::ode::RHS>.

B<Example>

    struct F:dm::ode::RHS{std::vector<double>evaluate(double,const std::vector<double>&y)override{return {-y[0]};}};F f;std::print("{}\n",f.evaluate(0,{1})[0]); // -1

Inherits from L<Datamunge::NativeObject|/Datamunge::NativeObject>.

Abstract in C++: subclass it in Perl to implement its virtual methods (C<use parent -norequire, 'Datamunge::Rhs'>): constructing the Perl subclass creates a C++ subclass whose virtual methods call the Perl methods, and C<SUPER::method> runs the C++ implementation. A subclass's C<new> must call C<SUPER::new>.

See also: L<Datamunge::OdeSolver|/Datamunge::OdeSolver>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::NativeObject');

=head2 new

    Datamunge::Rhs->new();

Calls the C++ constructor C<datamunge::ode::RHS>.

The object of a Perl subclass is a C++ subclass whose virtual methods call the Perl methods.

=cut

sub new {
    my $class = shift;
    my $self = bless {}, ref($class) || $class;
    my @args = @_;
    Datamunge::Rosetta::construct($self, 1396, ref($self) ne __PACKAGE__, \@args);
    return $self;
}

=head2 evaluate

    my $result = $rhs->evaluate($t, $y);

Evaluate a complete derivative vector at one actual time/state.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::ode::RHS::evaluate>.

B<Example>

    struct F:dm::ode::RHS{std::vector<double>evaluate(double,const std::vector<double>&y)override{return {-y[0]};}};F f;std::print("{}\n",f.evaluate(0,{2})[0]); // -2

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$t> (number)

Actual finite stage time supplied by solver.

=item C<$y> (array reference of number)

Actual finite fixed-dimensional stage,borrowed only for this call.

=back

Returns array reference of number: Owned finite derivative vector with the same dimension as y.

See also: C<ODESolver::solve()>.

=cut

sub evaluate {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(1395, [@_]);
}

