package Datamunge::OdeSolver;

=head1 Datamunge::OdeSolver

Owned-options forward solver with canonical one-step/adaptive/PECE paths.
Different solver objects own their options; concurrency also depends on the
caller's RHS thread safety. Methods use local work buffers and no global state.

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::ode::ODESolver>.

B<Example>

    dm::ode::ODESolver s;auto r=s.solve_builtin("exponential_decay",{1.},{1.},0.,.01);std::print("{}\n",r.t.back()); // 0.01

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

NIST DLMF3.7(v), Runge-Kutta Method, https://dlmf.nist.gov/3.7.v.

=item *

J. R. Dormand and P. J. Prince(1980), A family of embedded Runge-Kutta formulae, doi:10.1016/0771-050X(80)90013-3. Native controller does not reuse FSAL and rejects nonconforming tiny trials.

=back

Inherits from L<Datamunge::NativeObject|/Datamunge::NativeObject>.

See also: L<Datamunge::Rhs|/Datamunge::Rhs>, L<Datamunge::OdeTrace|/Datamunge::OdeTrace>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::NativeObject');

=head2 new

    Datamunge::OdeSolver->new($options);

Own and validate the complete original numerical options.

Calls the C++ constructor C<datamunge::ode::ODESolver>.

B<Example>

    dm::ode::ODESolver s;std::print("{}\n",s.options().step_size); // 0.01

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

NIST DLMF3.7(v), Runge-Kutta Method, https://dlmf.nist.gov/3.7.v.

=item *

J. R. Dormand and P. J. Prince(1980), A family of embedded Runge-Kutta formulae, doi:10.1016/0771-050X(80)90013-3. Native controller does not reuse FSAL and rejects nonconforming tiny trials.

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$options = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$options> (Datamunge::OdeOptions, optional)

Known method and finite admissible controls, defaults RK4/h.01.

=back

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If any complete method/numerical control fails.

See also: C<options()>.

=cut

sub new {
    my $class = shift;
    my $self = bless {}, ref($class) || $class;
    my @args = @_;
    Datamunge::Rosetta::construct($self, 1483, 0, \@args);
    return $self;
}

=head2 solve

    my $result = $ode_solver->solve($rhs, $y0, $t_start, $t_end);

Integrate one complete checked forward initial-value problem.
Complete finite nonempty y0 and finite forward span t_endE<gt>t_start with
representable difference are checked before callbacks. Known method, finite
positive step/abs_tol, finite rel_tolE<gt>=0 and max_stepE<gt>=0 are validated, including
inactive numeric controls. Each callback receives a finite fixed-dimensional
stage and must return finite matching dimension; exceptions propagate.
Steps must advance representable time; tiny/nonadvancing steps throw rather
than loop. No absolute1e-14 endpoint skip; successful output reaches t_end.
Adams uses original RK4 startup and clamped order2..4; a shortened final step
uses RK4 instead of applying constant-grid coefficients to unequal spacing.
RK45 does not reuse FSAL and accepts only scaled errorE<lt>=1, not a tiny-step bypass.
Local error control is not a certified global error, stability or energy bound.
State/control/input references are not retained. Callback effects before a
later error/quota failure persist; no partial solution/trace is returned.
Default work policies:1000000 attempts,7000000 RHS calls,1000000 retained
solution scalar entries; limits overloads allow0 to disable each policy.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::ode::ODESolver::solve>.

B<Example>

    struct F:dm::ode::RHS{std::vector<double>evaluate(double,const std::vector<double>&y)override{return {-y[0]};}};F f;dm::ode::ODESolver s({dm::ode::StepMethod::Euler,4,.25});std::print("{}\n",s.solve(f,{1.},0.,.25).y.back()[0]); // 0.75

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

NIST DLMF3.7(v), Runge-Kutta Method, https://dlmf.nist.gov/3.7.v.

=item *

J. R. Dormand and P. J. Prince(1980), A family of embedded Runge-Kutta formulae, doi:10.1016/0771-050X(80)90013-3. Native controller does not reuse FSAL and rejects nonconforming tiny trials.

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$rhs> (Datamunge::Rhs)

Live derivative callback, finite matching-dimensional outputs required.

=item C<$y0> (array reference of number)

Original finite nonempty fixed-dimensional state.

=item C<$t_start> (number)

Original finite initial time.

=item C<$t_end> (number)

Original finite strictly later terminal time.

=back

Returns Datamunge::OdeSolution: Owned actual ODESolution, including both requested endpoint times on success.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If complete controls/state/builtin or callback dimension fails.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Length> (C++ C<std::length_error>): If complete/actual count geometry or selected policies fail.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Arithmetic> (C++ C<std::overflow_error>): If a stage/derivative/proposal or advancing time is not representable.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::bad_alloc>): If owned storage allocation fails.

See also: L<Datamunge::OdeAttempt|/Datamunge::OdeAttempt>, L<Datamunge::OdeEvaluation|/Datamunge::OdeEvaluation>.

=cut

sub solve {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(1476, [@_]);
}

