package Datamunge::Adam;

=head1 Datamunge::Adam

Adam on the DifferentiableFunction callback interface.
Bias-corrected full-batch first and second moments, denominator sqrt(vhat)+epsilon. Tests the pre-update gradient norm after applying its update. No line search or general nonconvex convergence guarantee; O(p) reset local state.
The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed;
coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws.
Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable
objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone
is not a uniform convergence certificate.

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::optim::Adam>.

B<Example>

    const dm::optim::Adam optimizer;
    std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 10000

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

D. P. Kingma and J. Ba (2015), Adam: A Method for Stochastic Optimization, https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.6980

=back

Inherits from L<Datamunge::NativeObject|/Datamunge::NativeObject>.

See also: L<Datamunge::AdamOptions|/Datamunge::AdamOptions>, C<trace_optimize()>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::NativeObject');

=head2 new

    Datamunge::Adam->new($options);

Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.

Calls the C++ constructor C<datamunge::optim::Adam>.

B<Example>

    const dm::optim::Adam optimizer;
    std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 10000

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$options = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$options> (Datamunge::AdamOptions, optional)

Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by AdamOptions.

=back

See also: C<optimize()>.

=cut

sub new {
    my $class = shift;
    my $self = bless {}, ref($class) || $class;
    my @args = @_;
    Datamunge::Rosetta::construct($self, 1517, 0, \@args);
    return $self;
}

=head2 optimize

    my $result = $adam->optimize($function, \$coordinates);

Run Adam from the supplied point using the configured finite budget.
Bias-corrected full-batch first and second moments, denominator sqrt(vhat)+epsilon. Tests the pre-update gradient norm after applying its update. No line search or general nonconvex convergence guarantee; O(p) reset local state.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::optim::Adam::optimize>.

B<Example>

    dm::optim::QuadraticObjective f({2,8},{1,-2});
    dm::optim::AdamOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
    std::vector<double> x{0,0};
    const auto value=dm::optim::Adam(controls).optimize(f,x);
    std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$function> (Datamunge::DifferentiableFunction)

Borrowed DifferentiableFunction with matching finite output shapes.

=item C<$coordinates> (array reference of number, written back: pass a variable or a reference)

Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native returned point.

=back

Returns number: Objective at the native returned coordinates; not a stop-status code.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::exception>): Exceptions from supplied callbacks propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite inputs.

See also: C<trace_optimize()>, L<Datamunge::AdamOptions|/Datamunge::AdamOptions>.

=cut

sub optimize {
    my @args = @_;
    $args[2] = Datamunge::Rosetta::slot(0, $args[2]) if @args > 2;
    my %writebacks;
    my $result = Datamunge::Rosetta::call(1515, \@args, \%writebacks);
    Datamunge::Rosetta::write_back(\@_, 2, $writebacks{0}) if exists $writebacks{0};
    return $result;
}

