package Datamunge::Fista;

=head1 Datamunge::Fista

FISTA on the ProximalFunction callback interface.
Classical t recurrence (1+sqrt(1+4*t^2))/2 and extrapolated gradient points, followed by the supplied proximal map. Stops on successive accepted-coordinate displacement. No adaptive restart, backtracking or monotonicity safeguard. Supply the correct proximal dimensions and a rate at most 1/L for the standard convex bound.
The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed;
coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws.
Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable
objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone
is not a uniform convergence certificate.

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::optim::FISTA>.

B<Example>

    const dm::optim::FISTA optimizer;
    std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 10000

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

A. Beck and M. Teboulle (2009), A Fast Iterative Shrinkage-Thresholding Algorithm for Linear Inverse Problems, https://doi.org/10.1137/080716542

=back

Inherits from L<Datamunge::NativeObject|/Datamunge::NativeObject>.

See also: L<Datamunge::FistaOptions|/Datamunge::FistaOptions>, C<trace_optimize()>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::NativeObject');

=head2 new

    Datamunge::Fista->new($options);

Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.

Calls the C++ constructor C<datamunge::optim::FISTA>.

B<Example>

    const dm::optim::FISTA optimizer;
    std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 10000

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$options = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$options> (Datamunge::FistaOptions, optional)

Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by FISTAOptions.

=back

See also: C<optimize()>.

=cut

sub new {
    my $class = shift;
    my $self = bless {}, ref($class) || $class;
    my @args = @_;
    Datamunge::Rosetta::construct($self, 1782, 0, \@args);
    return $self;
}

=head2 optimize

    my $result = $fista->optimize($function, \$coordinates);

Run FISTA from the supplied point using the configured finite budget.
Classical t recurrence (1+sqrt(1+4*t^2))/2 and extrapolated gradient points, followed by the supplied proximal map. Stops on successive accepted-coordinate displacement. No adaptive restart, backtracking or monotonicity safeguard. Supply the correct proximal dimensions and a rate at most 1/L for the standard convex bound.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::optim::FISTA::optimize>.

B<Example>

    dm::optim::L1LeastSquaresObjective f({{1}},{3},1);
    dm::optim::FISTAOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
    std::vector<double> x{0};
    const auto value=dm::optim::FISTA(controls).optimize(f,x);
    std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$function> (Datamunge::ProximalFunction)

Borrowed ProximalFunction with matching finite output shapes.

=item C<$coordinates> (array reference of number, written back: pass a variable or a reference)

Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native returned point.

=back

Returns number: Objective at the native returned coordinates; not a stop-status code.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): FISTA: step_size must be positive.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::exception>): Exceptions from supplied callbacks propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite inputs.

See also: C<trace_optimize()>, L<Datamunge::FistaOptions|/Datamunge::FistaOptions>.

=cut

sub optimize {
    my @args = @_;
    $args[2] = Datamunge::Rosetta::slot(0, $args[2]) if @args > 2;
    my %writebacks;
    my $result = Datamunge::Rosetta::call(1780, \@args, \%writebacks);
    Datamunge::Rosetta::write_back(\@_, 2, $writebacks{0}) if exists $writebacks{0};
    return $result;
}

