=head1 Datamunge::GradientDescent

GradientDescent on the DifferentiableFunction callback interface.
Velocity is momentum*velocity-step_size*gradient. Updates before testing the gradient just used or absolute objective change. Equal objective values can stop an oscillation away from stationarity. O(p) reset velocity state; no line search.
The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent callbacks. Inputs are borrowed;
coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if a callback throws.
Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback exceptions; sharing a mutable
objective across threads requires external synchronisation. Normal return alone
is not a uniform convergence certificate.

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::optim::GradientDescent>.

B<Example>

    const dm::optim::GradientDescent optimizer;
    std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 10000

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

J. Nocedal and S. J. Wright (2006), Numerical Optimization, second edition, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-40065-5. The implemented variant and stopping rules are specified above.

=back

Inherits from L<Datamunge::NativeObject|/Datamunge::NativeObject>.

See also: L<Datamunge::GradientDescentOptions|/Datamunge::GradientDescentOptions>, C<trace_optimize()>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::NativeObject');

=head2 new

    Datamunge::GradientDescent->new($options);

Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.

Calls the C++ constructor C<datamunge::optim::GradientDescent>.

B<Example>

    const dm::optim::GradientDescent optimizer;
    std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 10000

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$options = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$options> (Datamunge::GradientDescentOptions, optional)

Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by GradientDescentOptions.

=back

See also: C<optimize()>.

=cut

sub new {
    my $class = shift;
    my $self = bless {}, ref($class) || $class;
    my @args = @_;
    Datamunge::Rosetta::construct($self, 1824, 0, \@args);
    return $self;
}

=head2 optimize

    my $result = $gradient_descent->optimize($function, \$coordinates);

Run GradientDescent from the supplied point using the configured finite budget.
Velocity is momentum*velocity-step_size*gradient. Updates before testing the gradient just used or absolute objective change. Equal objective values can stop an oscillation away from stationarity. O(p) reset velocity state; no line search.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::optim::GradientDescent::optimize>.

B<Example>

    dm::optim::QuadraticObjective f({2,8},{1,-2});
    dm::optim::GradientDescentOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
    std::vector<double> x{0,0};
    const auto value=dm::optim::GradientDescent(controls).optimize(f,x);
    std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$function> (Datamunge::DifferentiableFunction)

Borrowed DifferentiableFunction with matching finite output shapes.

=item C<$coordinates> (array reference of number, written back: pass a variable or a reference)

Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native returned point.

=back

Returns number: Objective at the native returned coordinates; not a stop-status code.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::exception>): Exceptions from supplied callbacks propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite inputs.

See also: C<trace_optimize()>, L<Datamunge::GradientDescentOptions|/Datamunge::GradientDescentOptions>.

=cut

sub optimize {
    my @args = @_;
    $args[2] = Datamunge::Rosetta::slot(0, $args[2]) if @args > 2;
    my %writebacks;
    my $result = Datamunge::Rosetta::call(1822, \@args, \%writebacks);
    Datamunge::Rosetta::write_back(\@_, 2, $writebacks{0}) if exists $writebacks{0};
    return $result;
}

=head2 options

    my $result = $gradient_descent->options();

Borrow this optimizer's stored configuration.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::optim::GradientDescent::options>.

Returns Datamunge::GradientDescentOptions: Const option reference, valid during this object's lifetime; no mutation.

=cut

sub options {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(1823, [@_]);
}

