package Datamunge::RbfGaussianProcessSurrogate;

=head1 Datamunge::RbfGaussianProcessSurrogate

Exact Gaussian-process regression surrogate with the squared-exponential kernel

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::optim::RBFGaussianProcessSurrogate>.

C<s\exp(-\lVert x-x'\rVert^2/2\ell^2)>.

fit() Cholesky-factors C<K+\sigma_n^2I=LL^\top> once and solves for
C<\alpha=(K+\sigma_n^2I)^{-1}(y-m)>; predict() then needs one forward substitution per point:
mean C<m+k_*^\top\alpha> and variance C<s-\lVert L^{-1}k_*\rVert^2>.

The two-argument constructor keeps the original fixed setup: zero prior mean, unit signal
variance, no normalisation or fitting, expected improvement. The options constructor adds
value normalisation, marginal-likelihood fitting of the hyperparameters and other acquisitions.
The kernel is isotropic, so the inputs should be on comparable scales.

B<Example>

    dm::optim::RBFGaussianProcessSurrogate gp(1.0, 0.0);
    gp.fit({{0.0}, {2.0}}, {1.0, -1.0});
    const auto p = gp.predict({1.0});
    std::print("{:.4f} {:.4f}\n", p.mean, p.sd);  // 0.0000 0.5933

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

C. E. Rasmussen and C. K. I. Williams (2006). *Gaussian Processes for Machine Learning*,
chapters 2 and 5. MIT Press.

=back

Inherits from L<Datamunge::BayesianSurrogate|/Datamunge::BayesianSurrogate>.

Subclass it in Perl to implement its virtual methods (C<use parent -norequire, 'Datamunge::RbfGaussianProcessSurrogate'>): constructing the Perl subclass creates a C++ subclass whose virtual methods call the Perl methods, and C<SUPER::method> runs the C++ implementation. A subclass's C<new> must call C<SUPER::new>.

See also: L<Datamunge::BayesianOptimization|/Datamunge::BayesianOptimization>, C<expected_improvement()>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::BayesianSurrogate');

