=head1 Datamunge::Bspline

B-spline design factory, analogous to R's splines::bs().
Owns a clamped basis on fixed boundaries, reused for training and prediction.
By default the first basis column is omitted, so a formula's intercept does
not duplicate the partition of unity. No fitting or smoothing is performed.
Const evaluations are independent and safe to run concurrently.

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::Bspline>.

B<Example>

    const dm::Bspline basis(0, 1, {0, 0.5, 1});
    const auto design = basis.eval({0, 0.5, 1});
    std::print("{} {}\n", design.rows(), design.cols()); // 3 4

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

C. de Boor (1972). "On calculating with B-splines". Journal of Approximation
Theory 6(1), 50-62. doi:10.1016/0021-9045(72)90080-9.

=back

Inherits from L<Datamunge::NativeObject|/Datamunge::NativeObject>.

See also: C<bs()>, C<fda::BSplineBasis>, C<stats::LM>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::NativeObject');

=head2 new

    Datamunge::Bspline->new($lower, $upper, $knots, $degree, $intercept);

Build a clamped basis from strictly increasing knot breakpoints.

Calls the C++ constructor C<datamunge::Bspline>.

B<Example>

    const dm::Bspline full(0, 1, {}, 1, true);
    std::print("{:.1f}\n", full.eval({0.5})(0, 0)); // 0.5

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$degree = 3>, C<$intercept = false>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$lower> (number)

Finite left boundary, strictly below upper.

=item C<$upper> (number)

Finite right boundary.

=item C<$knots> (array reference of number)

Finite increasing breakpoints inside [lower, upper]; boundary entries are optional and ignored when building interior knots. May be empty.

=item C<$degree> (integer, optional)

Polynomial degree, default 3 (cubic).

=item C<$intercept> (boolean, optional)

Include the first basis column, default false; use a no-intercept formula when true. Column count is interior knots + degree + (intercept ? 1 : 0).

=back

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): For invalid boundaries/knots or overflowing degree.

=cut

