package Datamunge::PSplineRegression;

=head1 Datamunge::PSplineRegression

A P-spline regression curve y ≈ Σ_j β_j B_j(x) (Eilers & Marx). C<dm::PSR> for short.

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::PSplineRegression>.

Minimizes ||y - Bβ||² + λ ||D_d β||², where D_d is the order-d difference matrix on the
coefficients (d = 2 by default). Uniform knots continue past both ends of the data
without repeated boundary knots. When d E<lt>= degree+1, polynomials of degree E<lt> d
go unpenalized: λ → ∞ gives the least-squares line for d = 2. A rich basis can
include functions with no data under them if the data identify the penalty nullspace.
Without an explicit λ, it is chosen
by minimizing generalized cross-validation, GCV(λ) = n·RSS / (n − edf)².

The polynomial-nullspace claim requires uniform spacing; it need not hold for irregular
supplied knots. A positive lambda identifies a rich basis only when the design identifies
the penalty nullspace. No values are dropped. The class owns its fit and permits concurrent
const reads. Sparse assembly and dense Cholesky cost O(n(p+1)^2+K^3); GCV repeats
this work. Very large lambda can make the equations numerically singular.

B<Example>

    const dm::PSR fit({0,1,2},{0,1,0},3,1.0,1,2);
    std::print("{:.6f} {:.6f}\n",fit.predict({1})[0],fit.effective_df()); // 0.428571 2.142857

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

P. H. C. Eilers and B. D. Marx (1996). Flexible smoothing with B-splines and penalties.
https://doi.org/10.1214/ss/1038425655

=back

Inherits from L<Datamunge::NativeObject|/Datamunge::NativeObject>.

See also: C<fda::fit_pspline()>, C<smoothing_path()>, C<summary()>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::NativeObject');

=head2 new

    Datamunge::PSplineRegression->new($x, $y, $n_basis, $lambda, $degree, $penalty_order);

C<n_basis> cubic B-splines with equally spaced knots over the range of C<x>; λ chosen
by GCV.
Searches log10(lambda) on [-8,8] in steps of 0.5, then uses golden-section refinement
around the best grid point. Singular candidates score infinity. This bounded refinement
does not guarantee a global continuous minimum. Penalty order is two.

Calls the C++ constructor C<datamunge::PSplineRegression>.

B<Example>

    const dm::Vector<> x=dm::seql(0,1,30);
    const dm::PSR fit(x,dm::sin(6*x),10);
    std::print("{}\n",fit.lambda()>0); // true

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

G. H. Golub, M. Heath and G. Wahba (1979). Generalized cross-validation as a
method for choosing a good ridge parameter. https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1979.10489751

=back

Overloads (C++ picks one by the arguments' types):

=over 4

=item *

C<Datamunge::PSplineRegression-E<gt>new($x (array reference), $y (array reference), $n_basis (integer))>

=item *

C<Datamunge::PSplineRegression-E<gt>new($x (array reference), $y (array reference), $n_basis (integer), $lambda (number), $degree (integer) = 3, $penalty_order (integer) = 2)>

=item *

C<Datamunge::PSplineRegression-E<gt>new($x (array reference), $y (array reference), $knots (array reference))>

=item *

C<Datamunge::PSplineRegression-E<gt>new($x (array reference), $y (array reference), $knots (array reference), $lambda (number), $degree (integer) = 3, $penalty_order (integer) = 2)>

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$x> (array reference)

Nonempty finite coordinates spanning a positive-width interval.

=item C<$y> (array reference)

Finite responses matching x.

=item C<$n_basis> (integer | array reference)

Basis count K, at least four.

=item C<$lambda> (number, optional)

Finite nonnegative multiplier of summed squared coefficient differences.

=item C<$degree> (integer, optional)

Polynomial degree, default three.

=item C<$penalty_order> (integer, optional)

Positive difference order, default two.

=back

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): For invalid data or basis size.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::runtime_error>): If all candidate systems fail numerically.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): For invalid data/options or non-finite extended knots.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::runtime_error>): If the penalized system is singular or non-finite.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Arithmetic> (C++ C<std::overflow_error>): If difference weights or the penalty overflow.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): For invalid data/knots or zero-width combined domain.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::runtime_error>): If every candidate system fails numerically.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): For invalid data, options, knots or zero-width combined domain.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::runtime_error>): If the system is singular or non-finite.

See also: C<gcv()>, C<smoothing_path()>.

=cut

