package Datamunge::CsvReader;

=head1 Datamunge::CsvReader

SWIG-friendly streaming CSV reader: returns a big file as DataFrames of at most
batch_size() rows each, so only one chunk is in memory at a time.

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::CsvReader>.

Wraps datasets::CsvReader; column types are fixed from the first 1000 rows.

B<Example>

    dm::CsvReader reader(dm::datasets::fetch("diamonds"), 10000);
    while (reader.has_next()) {
      std::unique_ptr<dm::DataFrame> chunk(reader.next());
      // ... use chunk ...
    }

Inherits from L<Datamunge::NativeObject|/Datamunge::NativeObject>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::NativeObject');

=head2 new

    Datamunge::CsvReader->new($path, $batch_size, $delimiter);

Opens C<path> and infers its column types.

Calls the C++ constructor C<datamunge::CsvReader>.

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$batch_size = 1000>, C<$delimiter = ','>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$path> (string)

The CSV file.

=item C<$batch_size> (integer, optional)

Rows per chunk; must be positive.

=item C<$delimiter> (string (one character), optional)

The field separator.

=back

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): if batch_size is not positive.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::runtime_error>): if the file cannot be opened or is malformed.

=cut

sub new {
    my $class = shift;
    my $self = bless {}, ref($class) || $class;
    my @args = @_;
    Datamunge::Rosetta::construct($self, 2446, 0, \@args);
    return $self;
}

