package Datamunge::Splom;

=head1 Datamunge::Splom

A scatterplot matrix (R's C<pairs()>, GGally's C<ggpairs()>) over the columns of a
data frame, with chainable setters; the SWIG-friendly facade for graphics::Splom.

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::Splom>.

Panel (row i, column j) puts variable j on x and variable i on y. The panel type follows
the two variables' kinds (numeric columns are quantitative, string columns categorical):

=over 4

=item *

diagonal: kernel density curves, one per group_by() group (numeric), or a stacked bar of
level counts (string), titled with the variable name;

=item *

numeric vs numeric above the diagonal: the points with a local linear smooth of the mean and
a 95% prediction band, one per group; point colors follow set_col() or group_by();

=item *

numeric vs numeric below the diagonal: bivariate kernel density contours, one set per group;

=item *

string vs numeric: one raincloud per level (half violin, box plot, and jittered points);

=item *

string vs string: a mosaic shaded by chi-squared Pearson residuals, with its p-value.

=back

Values are copied at construction, so the frame need not outlive the matrix. Rows with a
missing value in either variable are dropped from that panel only. Setters return
C<Splom&> for chaining; nothing is drawn until to_svg(), save(), or show(). The default
figure is 960 by 960 pixels with blue C<{37, 99, 235}> points of radius 2.5.

B<Example>

    const auto iris = dm::datasets::iris();
    const auto species = dm::factor(dm::c(iris.get_column<std::string>("Species").values()));
    const auto palette = dm::c(dm::RGB{255, 255, 80}, dm::RGB{100, 255, 255},
                               dm::RGB{220, 100, 255});
    auto pairs = dm::Splom(iris).set_title("Iris Pairs").set_col(species, palette);
    pairs.show();  // or pairs.save("iris_pairs.svg") without a display

Inherits from L<Datamunge::NativeObject|/Datamunge::NativeObject>.

See also: C<dm::splom()>, C<dm::Plot>, C<graphics::Splom>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::NativeObject');

=head2 new

    Datamunge::Splom->new($data, $columns);

Builds the matrix from a facade DataFrame (the overload SWIG bindings use).

Calls the C++ constructor C<datamunge::Splom>.

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$columns = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$data> (Datamunge::DataFrame)

Source frame; its values are copied.

=item C<$columns> (array reference of string, optional)

Columns to include in order; empty selects every numeric column plus every string column with at most graphics::Splom::max_default_levels distinct values.

=back

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): For a missing column, or fewer than two variables.

See also: C<Splom(const dstruct::DataFrame&>, C<const std::vectorE<lt>std::stringE<gt>&)>.

=cut

sub new {
    my $class = shift;
    my $self = bless {}, ref($class) || $class;
    my @args = @_;
    Datamunge::Rosetta::construct($self, 2517, 0, \@args);
    return $self;
}

