=head2 group_by

    my $result = $splom->group_by($column, $palette);

Splits every numeric panel by the groups of string column C<column> (GGally's
C<ggpairs(aes(colour = group))>); an empty palette uses the default level palette.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::Splom::group_by>.

The column need not be a panel variable but must be a string column of the source
frame. Groups are its sorted distinct non-missing values, and group k takes
C<palette[k % palette.size()]>. Each group gets its own points, diagonal density, smooth
and prediction band, and density contours; rows with a missing group are left out of
those panels. When the column is also a panel variable its rainclouds, bars, and mosaic axis
share the colors. Pass "" to clear the grouping. Clears point_colors().

B<Example>

    auto pairs = dm::Splom(dm::datasets::iris()).group_by("Species");
    std::print("{}\n", pairs.groups());  // ["setosa", "versicolor", "virginica"]

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$palette = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$column> (string)

String column to group by, or "" to clear.

=item C<$palette> (array reference of Datamunge::Rgb, optional)

Group colors in sorted-group order; empty uses the default.

=back

Returns Datamunge::Splom: This matrix, for chaining.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If C<column> is not a string column of the source frame.

See also: C<groups()>, C<set_col()>.

=cut

sub group_by {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(2496, [@_]);
}

