package Datamunge::HyperDual;

=head1 Datamunge::HyperDual

SWIG-friendly facade for datamunge::autodiff::HyperDual -- a second-order
forward-mode dual number carrying two independent derivative directions plus
their exact mixed second partial, for computing Hessian entries.

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::HyperDual>.

Inherits from L<Datamunge::NativeObject|/Datamunge::NativeObject>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::NativeObject');

=head2 new

    Datamunge::HyperDual->new($value, $eps1, $eps2, $eps1eps2);

A hyper-dual number value + eps1·ε₁ + eps2·ε₂ + eps1eps2·ε₁ε₂ (ε₁² = ε₂² = 0).

Calls the C++ constructor C<datamunge::HyperDual>.

Seed input i with eps1 = 1 and input j with eps2 = 1 (both on the same input for a
diagonal entry); the result's eps1eps2() is then the exact ∂²f/∂xᵢ∂xⱼ.

B<Example>

    const dm::HyperDual x(3.0, 1.0, 1.0);
    std::print("{}\n", x.multiply(x).eps1eps2());  // d²(x²)/dx² = 2

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$eps1 = 0.0>, C<$eps2 = 0.0>, C<$eps1eps2 = 0.0>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$value> (number)

Real part, the function value.

=item C<$eps1> (number, optional)

First derivative along the first seed direction (default 0).

=item C<$eps2> (number, optional)

First derivative along the second seed direction (default 0).

=item C<$eps1eps2> (number, optional)

Mixed second derivative part (default 0; leave 0 for inputs).

=back

=cut

sub new {
    my $class = shift;
    my $self = bless {}, ref($class) || $class;
    my @args = @_;
    Datamunge::Rosetta::construct($self, 3242, 0, \@args);
    return $self;
}

