}

package Datamunge::BSplineRefinement;

=head1 Datamunge::BSplineRefinement

A refined B-spline basis together with the operator that maps coarse
coefficients onto it.

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::fda::BSplineRefinement>.

For any coefficient vector c on the coarse basis, C<matrix * c> (equivalently apply())
gives coefficients on the refined basis that represent exactly the same spline.

B<Example>

    auto coarse = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(2, 4);
    auto refined = dm::fda::dyadic_refine(coarse);
    std::print("{}\n", refined.basis.basis_size()); // 6

Inherits from L<Datamunge::NativeObject|/Datamunge::NativeObject>.

See also: C<refine_basis()>, C<dyadic_refine()>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::NativeObject');

=head2 new

    Datamunge::BSplineRefinement->new();

Calls the C++ constructor C<datamunge::fda::BSplineRefinement>.

=cut

sub new {
    my $class = shift;
    my $self = bless {}, ref($class) || $class;
    my @args = @_;
    Datamunge::Rosetta::construct($self, 3327, 0, \@args);
    return $self;
}

=head2 apply

    my $result = $b_spline_refinement->apply($coefficients);

Maps coarse coefficients to the refined basis.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::fda::BSplineRefinement::apply>.

B<Example>

    auto r = dm::fda::dyadic_refine(dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(1, 2));
    std::print("{}\n", r.apply({0.0, 2.0})); // [0, 1, 2]

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$coefficients> (array reference of number)

One coefficient per coarse basis function.

=back

Returns array reference of number: One coefficient per refined basis function, representing the same spline.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If C<coefficients> does not match the coarse basis size.

=cut

sub apply {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(3324, [@_]);
}

=head2 basis

    my $value = $b_spline_refinement->basis;
    $b_spline_refinement->basis($value);

Refined basis (same degree and domain). (Datamunge::BSplineBasis).

=cut

sub basis {
    my $self = shift;
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(3325, [$self]) unless @_;
    Datamunge::Rosetta::call(3326, [$self, $_[0]]);
    return;
}

package Datamunge::TensorBSpline;

=head1 Datamunge::TensorBSpline

A scalar tensor-product B-spline f(x) = sum_j c_j B_j(x) with owned coefficients.

Wraps the C++ class C<datamunge::fda::TensorBSpline>.

With one basis this is an ordinary spline curve; with d bases a surface or volume. The
coefficient net is row-major over the per-axis basis sizes (last axis fastest), the same
column order as fda::BSpline::evaluate(). Evaluation clamps each coordinate into the
basis domain, so points outside the domain take the boundary value. Const methods are
safe to call concurrently while the object is not mutated.

B<Example>

    auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(1, 2);     // linear on [0, 1]
    dm::fda::TensorBSpline f({b, b}, {0, 1, 1, 2});         // f(x, y) = x + y
    std::print("{:.2f}\n", f.evaluate({0.25, 0.5}));        // 0.75

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

C. de Boor (1972). On calculating with B-splines. Journal of Approximation Theory
6(1), 50-62. https://doi.org/10.1016/0021-9045(72)90080-9

=back

Inherits from L<Datamunge::NativeObject|/Datamunge::NativeObject>.

See also: C<fit_tensor_bspline()>, C<fit_tensor_pspline()>, C<BSpline>.

=cut

our @ISA = ('Datamunge::NativeObject');

=head2 new

    Datamunge::TensorBSpline->new($basis, $coefficients);

One-dimensional spline curve on C<basis>.

Calls the C++ constructor C<datamunge::fda::TensorBSpline>.

B<Example>

    dm::fda::TensorBSpline f(dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(1, 2), {1.0, 3.0});
    std::print("{}\n", f.evaluate(0.5)); // 2

Overloads (C++ picks one by the arguments' types):

=over 4

=item *

C<Datamunge::TensorBSpline-E<gt>new($basis (Datamunge::BSplineBasis), $coefficients (array reference of number))>

=item *

C<Datamunge::TensorBSpline-E<gt>new($bases (array reference of Datamunge::BSplineBasis), $coefficients (array reference of number))>

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$basis> (Datamunge::BSplineBasis | array reference of Datamunge::BSplineBasis)

Basis for the single axis.

=item C<$coefficients> (array reference of number)

One coefficient per basis function.

=back

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If the coefficient count differs from basis.basis_size().

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If C<bases> is empty or the net size does not match.

=cut

