=head2 fit_cell

    $hierarchical_b_spline->fit_cell($cell, $bases, $points, $values, $options);

Least-squares fits a new patch on C<cell> so the whole hierarchy matches
C<values> at C<points>.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::fda::HierarchicalBSpline::fit_cell>.

The patch explains only what is left after every other patch on the path (the
residual values - evaluate(points)), in the spirit of Forsey-Bartels editing, so pass
raw data values rather than residuals. Points are global coordinates inside the cell.

B<Example>

    dm::fda::HierarchicalBSpline h({0.0}, {1.0});
    auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(1, 2);
    h.fit_cell(h.root(), {b}, {{0.0}, {1.0}}, {1.0, 3.0});
    std::print("{}\n", h.evaluate({0.5})); // 2

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$options = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$cell> (integer)

Cell id; must not already have a patch (clear_spline() first to refit).

