=head2 fit_level

    $truncated_hierarchical_b_spline->fit_level($level, $points, $values, $options);

Least-squares update of the active coefficients at C<level> only.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::fda::TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline::fit_level>.

Solves for the change of the level's active coefficients that best explains the
residual values - evaluate(points), using the exact truncated basis, so pass raw
data values. Points shallower than C<level> are unaffected by these functions and
are ignored. Typical use is coarse-to-fine: fit level 0, refine where residuals are
large, then fit the new level.

B<Example>

    auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(1, 2);
    dm::fda::TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline thb(dm::fda::TensorBSpline(b, {0.0, 0.0}));
    thb.fit_level(0, {{0.0}, {1.0}}, {1.0, 3.0});
    std::print("{}\n", thb.evaluate({0.5})); // 2

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$options = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$level> (integer)

Level with at least one active function.

=item C<$points> (array reference of array reference of number)

Sample locations inside the domain.

=item C<$values> (array reference of number)

One raw value per point.

=item C<$options> (Datamunge::SplineSolveOptions, optional)

Solver settings.

=back

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::OutOfRange> (C++ C<std::out_of_range>): For an invalid level.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): On empty/mismatched inputs, points outside the domain, or a level with no active functions.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::runtime_error>): If the normal equations are singular.

=cut

sub fit_level {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(3412, [@_]);
}

=head2 fit_level_penalized

    $truncated_hierarchical_b_spline->fit_level_penalized($level, $points, $values, $lambdas, $order, $options);

Penalized variant of fit_level(): adds the level's full-grid tensor difference
penalty restricted to its active functions, applied to the coefficient change.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::fda::TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline::fit_level_penalized>.

B<Example>

    auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(3, 8);
    dm::fda::TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline thb(dm::fda::TensorBSpline(b, std::vector<double>(8, 0.0)));
    thb.fit_level_penalized(0, {{0.0}, {1.0}}, {0.0, 1.0}, {1e3});
    std::print("{:.3f}\n", thb.evaluate({0.5})); // 0.500

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$order = 2>, C<$options = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$level> (integer)

Level.

=item C<$points> (array reference of array reference of number)

Sample locations.

=item C<$values> (array reference of number)

Raw values.

=item C<$lambdas> (array reference of number)

Nonnegative per-axis weights.

=item C<$order> (integer, optional)

Difference order (default 2).

=item C<$options> (Datamunge::SplineSolveOptions, optional)

Solver settings.

=back

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::OutOfRange> (C++ C<std::out_of_range>): For an invalid level.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): As for fit_level() and tensor_difference_penalty().

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::runtime_error>): If the normal equations are singular.

=cut

sub fit_level_penalized {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(3413, [@_]);
}

=head2 fit

    $truncated_hierarchical_b_spline->fit($points, $values, $options);

Least-squares fit of every active coefficient on every level at once
(replaces the current function).

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::fda::TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline::fit>.

B<Example>

    auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(1, 2);
    dm::fda::TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline thb(dm::fda::TensorBSpline(b, {0.0, 0.0}));
    thb.refine(thb.root(), 1);                      // adds a knot at 0.5
    thb.fit({{0.0}, {0.25}, {0.5}, {0.75}, {1.0}}, {1.0, 0.5, 0.0, 0.5, 1.0}); // |2x - 1|
    std::print("{:.3f}\n", thb.evaluate({0.75})); // 0.500

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$options = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$points> (array reference of array reference of number)

Sample locations inside the domain.

=item C<$values> (array reference of number)

One value per point.

=item C<$options> (Datamunge::SplineSolveOptions, optional)

Solver settings.

=back

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): On empty/mismatched inputs or points outside the domain.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::runtime_error>): If some active function has too little data (singular).

=cut

sub fit {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(3414, [@_]);
}

=head2 fit_penalized

    $truncated_hierarchical_b_spline->fit_penalized($points, $values, $lambdas, $order, $options);

Penalized all-level fit: adds lambda_k times the squared order-q differences
along axis k of every run of q + 1 neighbouring active coefficients of a level.

Calls the C++ method C<datamunge::fda::TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline::fit_penalized>.

Differences that would involve an inactive coefficient are omitted, so for q E<gt>= 1
constant coefficients (and, generally, polynomials of degree E<lt> q in the coefficient
index on each level) are unpenalized; q = 0 is ridge regression on all coefficients.

B<Example>

    auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(2, 6);
    dm::fda::TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline thb(dm::fda::TensorBSpline(b, std::vector<double>(6, 0.0)));
    thb.refine_region({0.0}, {0.5}, 1);
    thb.fit_penalized({{0.0}, {1.0}}, {2.0, 2.0}, {1.0}, 1);
    std::print("{:.3f}\n", thb.evaluate({0.3})); // 2.000

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$order = 2>, C<$options = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$points> (array reference of array reference of number)

Sample locations.

=item C<$values> (array reference of number)

One value per point.

=item C<$lambdas> (array reference of number)

Nonnegative per-axis weights.

=item C<$order> (integer, optional)

Difference order (default 2).

=item C<$options> (Datamunge::SplineSolveOptions, optional)

Solver settings.

=back

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): As for fit() and tensor_difference_penalty().

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::runtime_error>): If the normal equations are singular.

=cut

sub fit_penalized {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(3415, [@_]);
}

