=head2 value_colors

    my $result = value_colors($values, $palette, $z_min, $z_max);

One colour per value on a continuous scale, for colouring scatter points by a
numeric variable (ggplot2's C<scale_colour_viridis_c()>).

Calls C++ C<datamunge::graphics::value_colors>.

Each value is mapped linearly from [C<z_min>, C<z_max>] to the palette and rounded to the
nearest entry; values outside are clamped, NaN values get grey (190, 190, 190), and a zero
range maps everything to the middle colour. Pass the result to RPlot::points_colored().

B<Example>

    const auto cols = dm::graphics::value_colors({0.0, 0.5, 1.0}, dm::graphics::viridis_colors(3));
    std::print("{} {} {}\n", cols[0].r, cols[1].r, cols[2].r);  // 68 35 253

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$palette = C++ default>, C<$z_min = C++ default>, C<$z_max = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$values> (array reference of number)

Values to colour.

=item C<$palette> (array reference of Datamunge::Rgb, optional)

Colours from low to high (default viridis_colors(256)).

=item C<$z_min> (number, optional)

Value of the first colour; NaN (the default) uses the smallest value.

=item C<$z_max> (number, optional)

Value of the last colour; NaN (the default) uses the largest value.

=back

