=head2 leapfrog_gaussian_acceptance

    my $result = leapfrog_gaussian_acceptance($step_size, $num_steps, $sd, $angles);

Exact expected Metropolis acceptance of one HMC proposal (fixed step size and
number of leapfrog steps, unit mass) on a one-dimensional

Calls C++ C<datamunge::bayes::leapfrog_gaussian_acceptance>.

C<N(m,s^2)> target, started
in equilibrium.

In scaled coordinates C<x=((q-m)/s,\,p)> each leapfrog step is the linear map
C<M=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\-h/2&1\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}1&h\\0&1\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\-h/2&1\end{pmatrix}>
with C<h=\varepsilon/s>, C<\det M=1> and C<\operatorname{tr}M=2-h^2>, so it is stable
exactly when C<\varepsilonE<lt>2s>. Writing C<x_0=r(\cos\phi,\sin\phi)> the energy error is
C<\Delta H=r^2f(\phi)> with C<f=\tfrac12(|M^L(\cos\phi,\sin\phi)|^2-1)>, and because
C<r^2/2\sim\text{Exp}(1)> the radial average of C<\min(1,e^{-\Delta H})> is
C<1/(1+2f)> for C<fE<gt>0> and 1 otherwise. The angle is integrated numerically.

C<\varepsilonE<gt>0>.

C<L\geq1>.

C<sE<gt>0>.

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$angles = 4096>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$step_size> (number)

Leapfrog step size

=item C<$num_steps> (integer)

Number of leapfrog steps

=item C<$sd> (number)

Target standard deviation

=item C<$angles> (integer, optional)

Number of quadrature angles (at least 8).

=back

Returns number: The expected acceptance probability.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): for non-positive arguments.

=cut

sub leapfrog_gaussian_acceptance {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(63, [@_]);
}

=head2 gaussian_importance_efficiency

    my $result = gaussian_importance_efficiency($scale, $dimension);

Asymptotic effective-sample-size fraction

Calls C++ C<datamunge::bayes::gaussian_importance_efficiency>.

C<N_{\text{eff}}/N> of importance sampling
a C<d>-dimensional Gaussian target with a Gaussian proposal of the same mean and
covariance scaled by C<k^2>.

C<N_{\text{eff}}/N\to1/E_q[w^2]> for weights C<w=p/q> normalised to mean 1. In one
dimension C<E_q[w^2]=\int p^2/q=k^2/\sqrt{2k^2-1}>, finite only for C<k^2E<gt>1/2>, and the
dimensions multiply, giving C<\bigl(\sqrt{2k^2-1}/k^2\bigr)^d>. It is 1 at C<k=1> and
0 (infinite weight variance) for C<k\le1/\sqrt2>.

C<kE<gt>0>.

C<d\ge1>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$scale> (number)

Proposal scale factor

=item C<$dimension> (integer)

Dimension

=back

Returns number: The efficiency in [0, 1].

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): for non-positive C<scale> or C<dimension>.

=cut

sub gaussian_importance_efficiency {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(64, [@_]);
