=head2 dog_blobs

    my $result = dog_blobs($img, $num_scales, $sigma0, $threshold);

Difference-of-Gaussians blob detection (the detector SIFT keypoints are built on,
without the orientation/descriptor stages): builds C<num_scales> + 1 Gaussian-
blurred copies of C<img> at geometrically-spaced sigma_i = C<sigma0> * 2^(i /
num_scales), subtracts consecutive pairs to get C<num_scales> DoG images, then keeps
every point that is a strict extremum (min or max) among its 26 neighbors in the
resulting (x, y, scale) volume -- 8 in its own DoG layer plus 9 in each adjacent
layer -- and whose |response| exceeds C<threshold>. A single octave only (no
image-halving between octaves like full SIFT) -- simpler, and sufficient for
detecting blobs across a single order-of-magnitude size range.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::cv::dog_blobs>.

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$num_scales = 4>, C<$sigma0 = 1.6>, C<$threshold = 3.0>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$img> (Datamunge::Image)

=item C<$num_scales> (integer, optional)

=item C<$sigma0> (number, optional)

=item C<$threshold> (number, optional)

=back

Returns array reference of Datamunge::KeyPoint.

=cut

sub dog_blobs {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(73, [@_]);
}

