=head2 lucas_kanade_optical_flow

    my $result = lucas_kanade_optical_flow($prev, $next, $points, $window_radius);

Sparse Lucas-Kanade optical flow: for each of C<points>, solves the classical 2x2
windowed least-squares system (built from Sobel spatial gradients Ix/Iy on C<prev>
and the temporal gradient It = next - prev, both accumulated over a
(2*window_radius+1)^2 window) for the local translation (dx, dy) that best explains
the window's brightness change under the standard optical-flow brightness-constancy
assumption. Single-level (not the pyramidal coarse-to-fine extension real
implementations use for large motions) -- correctly handles motions up to roughly
C<window_radius> pixels, which is this module's documented scope tradeoff; use
gaussian_pyramid() externally and call this once per level if larger motions matter.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::cv::lucas_kanade_optical_flow>.

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$window_radius = 7>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$prev> (Datamunge::Image)

=item C<$next> (Datamunge::Image)

=item C<$points> (array reference of array reference [integer, integer])

=item C<$window_radius> (integer, optional)

=back

Returns array reference of Datamunge::FlowVector.

=cut

sub lucas_kanade_optical_flow {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(88, [@_]);
}

