=head2 delaunay_triangulation

    my $result = delaunay_triangulation($points);

Construct a complete unconstrained Delaunay triangulation with exact signs.
Retains the original one-vector signature and Triangle layout. Finite coordinate
validation precedes even trivial results. Duplicates coalesce to earliest ids;
collinear/fewer-than-three distinct sites return no faces. Uses Bowyer-Watson
cavity replacement with symbolic hull ghosts, avoiding any finite super-triangle
whose proximity could remove valid narrow faces. All orientation/incircle signs
are exact for finite stored binary64, including subnormals/extreme scales.
Cocircular ties retain existing faces under the ordering in DelaunayTrace.
Faces are strictly CCW, contain no ghosts and reference original ids. Finite
output order is canonical as documented on DelaunayTrace. No boundary constraints,
holes, Steiner refinement or minimum-angle guarantee is supplied. Input unchanged;
O(n log(n+1)+u^2 log(u+1)) worst-case work for n records/u distinct sites with
ordered cavity maps and full live scans; O(n+u) working/output storage, no shared
state or optional resource caps. Exact-predicate cost is bounded per binary64 query.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::geometry::delaunay_triangulation>.

B<Example>

    std::print("{}\n",dm::geometry::delaunay_triangulation({{0,0},{1,0},{.5,.000001}}).size()); // 1

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

A. Bowyer (1981), Computing Dirichlet tessellations, Computer Journal 24(2),162–166, doi:10.1093/comjnl/24.2.162.

=item *

D. F. Watson (1981), Computing the n-dimensional Delaunay tessellation with application to Voronoi polytopes, Computer Journal 24(2),167–172, doi:10.1093/comjnl/24.2.167. This variant uses symbolic boundary faces, exact decoded-dyadic signs, earliest duplicate identities and ordered cavity edges.

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$points> (array reference of Datamunge::Point2D)

Borrowed finite planar sites, possibly empty/duplicate/collinear.

=back

Returns array reference of Datamunge::Triangle: Owned complete finite mesh of the distinct sites' convex hull, or no faces for collinearity.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Any nonfinite coordinate, even on short input.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Length> (C++ C<std::length_error>): Counter arithmetic or container capacity exceeded.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::logic_error>): Internal cavity topology invariant fails; no partial mesh returned.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::bad_alloc>): Allocation fails.

See also: C<delaunay_trace()>, C<incircle_sign()>.

=cut

sub delaunay_triangulation {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(121, [@_]);
}

=head2 delaunay_trace

    my $result = delaunay_trace($points, $max_points, $max_tests, $max_entries);

Capture actual symbolic-boundary cavity transitions from the same solver.
No replay. Full finite validation precedes the original-record cap and all
allocation. max_tests bounds cumulative incircle plus hull-side calls, checked
before each complete live-face scan. Trace entries charge one per insertion
plus removed faces, boundary edges and added faces, including ghosts, checked
before retaining the event. Points/maps/final output and temporary cavities are
excluded from that entry count; caps do not bound bytes or wall time. Zero
disables only an optional cap. O(sum cavity/event sizes) additional history,
at worst O(u^2), beyond the ordinary solver. Failure returns no partial result.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::geometry::delaunay_trace>.

B<Example>

    const auto t=dm::geometry::delaunay_trace({{0,0},{4,0},{4,4},{0,4},{2,2}},5,10);
    std::print("{} {}\n",t.triangles.size(),t.work.incircle_tests+t.work.hull_side_tests); // 4 10

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

Bowyer (1981), doi:10.1093/comjnl/24.2.162; Watson (1981), doi:10.1093/comjnl/24.2.167; records the symbolic-boundary variant rather than a geometric replay.

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$max_points = 1000000>, C<$max_tests = 10000000>, C<$max_entries = 1000000>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$points> (array reference of Datamunge::Point2D)

Finite original coordinates, unchanged.

=item C<$max_points> (integer, optional)

Complete original-record cap, default 1000000; zero disables.

=item C<$max_tests> (integer, optional)

Cumulative complete-scan conflict cap, default 10000000; zero disables.

=item C<$max_entries> (integer, optional)

Cumulative event-entry cap, default 1000000; zero disables.

=back

Returns Datamunge::DelaunayTrace: Owned points, original-id maps, finite mesh, actual events and work.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Any nonfinite coordinate.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Length> (C++ C<std::length_error>): Optional cap, count arithmetic or container capacity exceeded.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::logic_error>): Internal cavity invariant fails.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::bad_alloc>): Allocation fails.

See also: L<Datamunge::DelaunayTrace|/Datamunge::DelaunayTrace>, C<delaunay_triangulation()>.

=cut

sub delaunay_trace {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(122, [@_]);
}

=head2 delaunay_analyze

    my $result = delaunay_analyze($points, $triangles, $max_tests);

Measure a supplied mesh and test every nonvertex site against every circle.
Performs the structural checks on DelaunayAnalysis first. The complete prospective
circle count faces*(unique_sites-3) is checked before circle queries; zero cap
disables only that limit. O(n log(n+1)+f log(f+1)+fu) work and O(n+f) storage;
minimum angles are floating diagnostics and are not certified bounds. This is a
local property analysis, not a global embedding/coverage certificate.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::geometry::delaunay_analyze>.

B<Example>

    const std::vector<dm::geometry::Point2D> p{{0,0},{1,0},{1,1},{0,1}};
    const auto a=dm::geometry::delaunay_analyze(p,dm::geometry::delaunay_triangulation(p));
    std::print("{} {} {}\n",a.edges,a.boundary_edges,a.circle_tests); // 5 4 2

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$max_tests = 1000000>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$points> (array reference of Datamunge::Point2D)

Finite original sites, unchanged.

=item C<$triangles> (array reference of Datamunge::Triangle)

Finite CCW faces with valid original indices, unchanged.

=item C<$max_tests> (integer, optional)

Complete exact-circle query cap, default 1000000; zero disables.

=back

Returns Datamunge::DelaunayAnalysis: Owned scalar topology, actual query/violation counts and approximate angle.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Nonfinite coordinates, bad/degenerate/non-CCW/duplicate faces or nonmanifold edges.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Length> (C++ C<std::length_error>): Circle-count arithmetic or optional cap exceeded.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::bad_alloc>): Allocation fails.

See also: L<Datamunge::DelaunayAnalysis|/Datamunge::DelaunayAnalysis>, C<delaunay_triangulation()>.

=cut

sub delaunay_analyze {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(123, [@_]);
}

=head2 delaunay_uniform_cloud

    my $result = delaunay_uniform_cloud($count, $seed, $extent, $max_points);

Generate the report's seeded uniform square-cloud control in the core.
Uses mt19937(seed) and uniform_real_distributionE<lt>doubleE<gt>(0,extent), x then y
for each point. Same-library runs reproduce the original report's seed20250728
and 30-site square when defaults are used. Standard distribution algorithms
need not match across C++ libraries, so the seed is not a universal byte fixture.
Coincident draws are allowed and later triangulation deduplicates them.
O(count) work/storage, owned output, no global RNG or shared mutation.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::geometry::delaunay_uniform_cloud>.

B<Example>

    std::print("{}\n",dm::geometry::delaunay_uniform_cloud(30).size()); // 30

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$seed = 20250728>, C<$extent = 1e+01>, C<$max_points = 1000000>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$count> (integer)

Number of original generated records, zero allowed.

=item C<$seed> (integer, optional)

mt19937 seed, default 20250728.

=item C<$extent> (number, optional)

Positive finite square extent, default 10; checked even for count zero.

=item C<$max_points> (integer, optional)

Optional complete output-record cap, default 1000000; zero disables.

=back

Returns array reference of Datamunge::Point2D: Owned generated points in [0,extent) on each axis.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Nonpositive or nonfinite extent.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Length> (C++ C<std::length_error>): Optional count cap or container capacity exceeded before allocation.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::bad_alloc>): Allocation fails.

See also: C<delaunay_trace()>.

=cut

sub delaunay_uniform_cloud {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(124, [@_]);
}

