=head2 closest_pair

    my $result = closest_pair($points);

Return the closest point copies and finite-double distance using the checked all-pairs kernel.
Validates at least two finite points, visits every original iE<lt>j pair and compares
widened hypot distances. Equal rounded distances keep the lexicographically
first index pair. Duplicates are valid distinct records and can yield zero.
No early stop, sorting or spatial pruning; O(n^2) work and O(1) auxiliary storage.
No input mutation/shared state. Widened comparison can change former false
zero/infinite results and last-bit ties; it is not an exact-real predicate.
The old call/result layout remains; indexed/traced variants retain input ids.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::geometry::closest_pair>.

B<Example>

    const auto r=dm::geometry::closest_pair({{0,0},{3,4},{0,1}});
    std::print("{}\n",r.distance); // 1

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$points> (array reference of Datamunge::Point2D)

Borrowed original finite planar coordinates, length at least two.

=back

Returns Datamunge::ClosestPairResult: Owned selected point copies and checked finite-double measured distance.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If point count is too small or any coordinate is nonfinite.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Length> (C++ C<std::length_error>): If complete pair count cannot fit size_t.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Arithmetic> (C++ C<std::overflow_error>): If arithmetic/counters or the selected legacy distance cannot fit.

See also: C<closest_pair_indexed()>, C<closest_pair_trace()>.

=cut

sub closest_pair {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(129, [@_]);
}

=head2 convex_hull

    my $result = convex_hull($points);

Compute Andrew's strict monotone-chain hull using exact binary64 orientation signs.
Validates all coordinates before internal indexed copies or sorting; the
original by-value argument copy occurs at the call boundary. Exact coordinate
equality removes duplicates (including signed zero); the earliest matching
original record supplies its representation. Returns lexicographic-minimum
first, counterclockwise, no repeated endpoint and no collinear edge interiors.
Empty/singleton/two-point/collinear outputs follow ConvexHullAnalysis. O(n log n)
comparisons plus O(n) bounded exact-sign tests, O(n) scratch/output, no global
state. The legacy by-value signature is retained and has no optional caps.
Other geometry routines that use cross() retain their own floating contracts.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::geometry::convex_hull>.

B<Example>

    const auto h=dm::geometry::convex_hull({{0,0},{2,0},{2,2},{0,2},{1,1}});
    std::cout << h.size(); // 4

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

A. M. Andrew (1979), Another efficient algorithm for convex hulls in two
dimensions, https://doi.org/10.1016/0020-0190(79)90072-3 .

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$points> (array reference of Datamunge::Point2D)

Finite planar records, copied by value as in the original API.

=back

Returns array reference of Datamunge::Point2D: Owned strict hull of the represented coordinates.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Any nonfinite coordinate.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Length> (C++ C<std::length_error>): Work/container size exhaustion.

See also: C<convex_hull_analyze()>, C<convex_hull_trace()>, C<orientation_sign()>.

=cut

sub convex_hull {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(130, [@_]);
}

=head2 convex_hull_analyze

    my $result = convex_hull_analyze($points, $options);

Execute the same exact-sign hull kernel with identities and actual work.
Same ordering, degeneracy and complexity contract as convex_hull(). Full finite
validation precedes the complete max_points cap and scratch allocation. No
events or completed-chain snapshots are retained; event cap is ignored.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::geometry::convex_hull_analyze>.

B<Example>

    const auto a=dm::geometry::convex_hull_analyze({{0,0},{1,0},{2,0}});
    std::cout << a.hull.size() << ' ' << a.work.distinct_points; // 2 3

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

Andrew (1979), https://doi.org/10.1016/0020-0190(79)90072-3 .

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$options = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$points> (array reference of Datamunge::Point2D)

Finite original records; not mutated.

=item C<$options> (Datamunge::ConvexHullOptions, optional)

Complete original-record cap, zero unlimited; defaults one million.

=back

Returns Datamunge::ConvexHullAnalysis: Owned hull, earliest original indices and actual counters.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Nonfinite coordinate.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Length> (C++ C<std::length_error>): Complete point cap or work/container exhaustion.

See also: C<convex_hull()>, L<Datamunge::ConvexHullOptions|/Datamunge::ConvexHullOptions>.

=cut

sub convex_hull_analyze {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(131, [@_]);
}

=head2 convex_hull_trace

    my $result = convex_hull_trace($points, $options);

Capture the hull kernel's actual stack events without replay or quadratic snapshots.
Same computation/work as analyze, plus O(n) owned sorted records, compact
test/pop/push edits and completed lower/upper chains. Positive max_events is
checked online on actual records, including pushes; zero disables the cap.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::geometry::convex_hull_trace>.

B<Example>

    const auto t=dm::geometry::convex_hull_trace({{0,0},{1,0}});
    std::cout << t.events.size(); // 4

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

Andrew (1979), https://doi.org/10.1016/0020-0190(79)90072-3 .

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$options = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$points> (array reference of Datamunge::Point2D)

Finite original records; not mutated.

=item C<$options> (Datamunge::ConvexHullOptions, optional)

Complete point and actual event caps.

=back

Returns Datamunge::ConvexHullTrace: Owned actual sorted coordinates, identities, work and scan edits.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Nonfinite coordinate.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Length> (C++ C<std::length_error>): Point/event cap or work/container exhaustion.

See also: C<convex_hull_analyze()>, L<Datamunge::HullEvent|/Datamunge::HullEvent>.

