=head2 triangulate_polygon

    my $result = triangulate_polygon($polygon);

Triangulate a finite simple polygon using exact orientation-based ear decisions.
Rejects duplicate vertices, zero area, overlapping adjacent edges and every
nonadjacent edge intersection/touch. A repeated closing vertex is a duplicate.
Collinear forward boundary vertices are allowed. Uses the exact turn at the
lexicographically least vertex for simple-polygon winding, avoiding area
cancellation. O(n^2) validation and O(n^3) worst-case ear search, O(n) working
storage. nE<lt>3 returns empty after finite validation. For n=3 the historical
vertex order is retained; larger clockwise rings are reversed internally.
Input is borrowed unchanged; no references or mutable global state survive.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::geometry::triangulate_polygon>.

B<Example>

    std::print("{}\n",dm::geometry::triangulate_polygon({{0,0},{2,0},{2,2},{0,2}}).size()); // 2

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

D. Eberly, Triangulation by Ear Clipping, https://www.geometrictools.com/Documentation/TriangulationByEarClipping.pdf .
This implementation uses exact finite-binary64 orientation predicates.

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$polygon> (array reference of Datamunge::Point2D)

Finite simple boundary in either winding, without duplicate closing vertex.

=back

Returns array reference of Datamunge::Triangle: Owned original-index triangles; valid nE<gt>=3 produces exactly n-2.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If coordinates or polygon geometry are invalid.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Length> (C++ C<std::length_error>): If default predicate policy or representable storage is exceeded.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::runtime_error>): If exact ear search cannot finish a validated polygon.

=cut

sub triangulate_polygon {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(161, [@_]);
}

