=head2 expected_improvement

    my $result = expected_improvement($mean, $sd, $incumbent, $exploration);

Expected improvement of a Gaussian prediction below an incumbent (minimisation).

Calls C++ C<datamunge::optim::expected_improvement>.

For C<Y\sim N(\mu,\sigma^2)> and target C<t=\text{incumbent}-\xi>,
C<\operatorname{E}\max(t-Y,0)=(t-\mu)\Phi(u)+\sigma\varphi(u)> with C<u=(t-\mu)/\sigma>.

C<\mu>.

C<\sigma\ge0>; 0 gives C<\max(t-\mu,0)>.

C<\xi\ge0> an improvement must exceed.

B<Example>

    std::print("{:.4f}\n", dm::optim::expected_improvement(0.0, 1.0, 0.0));  // 0.3989 = phi(0)

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$exploration = 0.0>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$mean> (number)

Predicted mean

=item C<$sd> (number)

Predicted standard deviation

=item C<$incumbent> (number)

Smallest value observed so far.

=item C<$exploration> (number, optional)

Margin

=back

Returns number: The expected improvement (never negative).

=cut

