=head2 p_adjust

    my $result = p_adjust($p, $method);

Adjust a vector of p-values for multiple comparisons, matching R's p.adjust().

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::p_adjust>.

=over 4

=item *

Bonferroni: p[i] * n, capped at 1 -- simplest and most conservative FWER control.

=item *

Holm: step-down Bonferroni (Holm-Bonferroni) -- uniformly more powerful than
plain Bonferroni while still controlling FWER, no extra assumptions.

=item *

Hochberg: step-up FWER control -- more powerful than Holm, but assumes the test
statistics are independent (or positively dependent).

=item *

Hommel: step-up FWER control, uniformly more powerful than Hochberg under the
same independence assumption; the most expensive to compute (O(n^2)). For n == 2
this degenerates to (and is computed as) Hochberg, matching R.

=item *

BH (Benjamini-Hochberg, aka "fdr" in R): controls the false discovery rate under
independence or positive regression dependence -- the standard choice for large-
scale testing (e.g. genomics, many simultaneous hypothesis tests).

=item *

BY (Benjamini-Yekutieli): controls the FDR under arbitrary dependence; more
conservative than BH.

=item *

None: returns the input p-values unchanged.

=back

As in R, a single p-value (n E<lt>= 1) is always returned unchanged regardless of
C<method>.

Overloads (C++ picks one by the arguments' types):

=over 4

=item *

C<p_adjust($p (array reference of number), $method (Datamunge::PAdjustMethod constant) = Datamunge::PAdjustMethod::BH) returning array reference of number>

=item *

C<p_adjust($p (array reference of number), $method (string)) returning array reference of number>

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$p> (array reference of number)

p-values in [0, 1].

=item C<$method> (Datamunge::PAdjustMethod constant | string, optional)

Method name, e.g. "bonferroni", "holm", "BH".

=back

Returns array reference of number.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): An unknown method name.

=cut

sub p_adjust {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(190, [@_]);
}

=head2 p_adjust_method

    my $result = p_adjust_method($name);

Parses an adjustment method name, like R's p.adjust.methods.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::p_adjust_method>.

Case-insensitive. Accepts R's names "holm", "hochberg", "hommel", "bonferroni", "BH", "BY",
"fdr" (an alias of BH, as in R) and "none". Tukey's HSD is not a p-value adjustment (it
needs the group means, sizes and pooled variance), so it is not accepted here.

B<Example>

    const bool holm = dm::stats::p_adjust_method("Holm") == dm::stats::PAdjustMethod::Holm;
    std::print("{}\n", holm);   // true

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/p.adjust.html

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$name> (string)

Method name.

=back

Returns Datamunge::PAdjustMethod constant: The matching PAdjustMethod.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): An unknown name; the message lists the accepted names.

See also: C<p_adjust()>.

=cut

sub p_adjust_method {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(191, [@_]);
}

=head2 westfall_young_adjust

    my $result = westfall_young_adjust($resampled_p, $sorted_p);

Westfall-Young single-step minP adjustment (Westfall & Young, 1993) -- a resampling-
based FWER correction that accounts for the actual dependence structure between
tests (unlike the analytic methods above, which either assume independence or make
no assumption at all).

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::westfall_young_adjust>.

C<resampled_p> is a B x m matrix: row b holds the m p-values recomputed on the
b-th permutation or bootstrap resample (generated by the caller -- this function is
purely the combinatorial engine, not the resampling scheme itself). C<sorted_p> is
the m original p-values sorted ascending, and column k of every row in C<resampled_p>
must correspond to the same hypothesis as sorted_p[k] (i.e. the caller sorts once and
reorders both consistently before calling).

Single-step: every hypothesis is compared against the same reference distribution --
the per-resample minimum p-value taken over *all* m hypotheses -- so adjusted p[k] =
(fraction of resamples whose overall minimum p-value E<lt>= sorted_p[k]). This is
automatically monotonic in sorted_p and valid under subset pivotality, but less
powerful than the step-down refinement below since it never narrows the comparison
set as hypotheses are resolved.

Returned in the same (ascending sorted_p) order as the input; the caller is
responsible for mapping back to original hypothesis order if needed.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$resampled_p> (array reference of array reference of number)

=item C<$sorted_p> (array reference of number)

=back

Returns array reference of number.

=cut

sub westfall_young_adjust {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(192, [@_]);
}

=head2 romano_wolf_adjust

    my $result = romano_wolf_adjust($resampled_p, $sorted_p);

Romano-Wolf step-down resampling adjustment (Romano & Wolf, 2005) -- like
westfall_young_adjust, but more powerful: at each step k (in ascending sorted_p
order), the reference distribution is the per-resample minimum p-value taken only
over the *remaining* hypotheses k..m-1, narrowing by one hypothesis at each step
rather than always using all m. This is the step-down generalization that controls
FWER without requiring subset pivotality. Monotonicity (adjusted p[k] E<gt>= adjusted
p[k-1]) is enforced explicitly, since narrowing the comparison set at each step means
it isn't automatic the way it is for the single-step procedure above.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::romano_wolf_adjust>.

Same input/output conventions as westfall_young_adjust: C<resampled_p> is a B x m
matrix with column k aligned to sorted_p[k], and the result is returned in the same
sorted order.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$resampled_p> (array reference of array reference of number)

=item C<$sorted_p> (array reference of number)

=back

Returns array reference of number.

=cut

sub romano_wolf_adjust {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(193, [@_]);
}

=head2 one_way_anova

    my $result = one_way_anova($values, $group_sizes);

Classical equal-variance one-way ANOVA, like R oneway.test(var.equal=TRUE).

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::one_way_anova>.

Tests equality of group means with between/within mean-square ratio. Independent normal
observations and a common within-group population variance give the exact F reference. This is
not Welch ANOVA. O(N) work, O(1) auxiliary storage; input unchanged, no omission or shared
mutable state.

B<Example>

    const auto a=dm::stats::one_way_anova({1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9},{3,3,3});
    std::print("{:.0f}\n",a.statistic); // 27

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/oneway.test.html

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$values> (array reference of number)

Finite observations concatenated group by group.

=item C<$group_sizes> (array reference of integer)

At least two positive sizes summing exactly to values.size(); N must exceed the group count.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: statistic=F, parameter1=k-1, parameter2=N-k and upper-tail p_value; no CI or group estimates.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Invalid grouping/nonfinite input, no residual df, zero within- group variation or arithmetic overflow.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub one_way_anova {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(194, [@_]);
}

=head2 welch_anova

    my $result = welch_anova($values, $group_sizes);

Welch's one-way test of equal means in independent normal groups with unequal variances.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::welch_anova>.

Weights means by inverse estimated sampling variances and uses an approximate F reference.
Inputs are unchanged. Work is linear in observations with O(k) storage for k groups.

B<Example>

    const auto fit = dm::stats::welch_anova({1, 2, 4, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 3, 4, 5, 5}, {3, 5, 4});
    std::print("{:.4f}\n", fit.statistic); // 4.1238

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

Welch (1951), On the Comparison of Several Mean Values, doi:10.2307/2332579.

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$values> (array reference of number)

Finite observations concatenated by group.

=item C<$group_sizes> (array reference of integer)

At least two groups of size two or larger, each with positive variance.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: F statistic, numerator and denominator degrees of freedom, and upper-tail p-value.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Invalid sizes, nonfinite observations, zero variance or overflow.

See also: C<one_way_anova()>, C<games_howell()>.

=cut

sub welch_anova {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(195, [@_]);
}

=head2 games_howell

    my $result = games_howell($values, $group_sizes, $confidence_level);

Compares all independent normal group means using Games–Howell intervals and p-values.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::games_howell>.

Allows unequal group sizes and variances. Uses a pair-specific variance and degrees of freedom
