=head2 kruskal_wallis_test

    my $result = kruskal_wallis_test($values, $group_sizes);

Tie-corrected Kruskal-Wallis rank test, like R kruskal.test().

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::kruskal_wallis_test>.

Ranks the pooled observations with average ties and uses the chi-square(k-1) approximation.
The usual exchangeability null is identical independent group distributions; a common-shape
shift model permits a location interpretation. It is not an omnibus test for every possible
distributional difference. Small groups can invalidate the approximation. O(N log N) work/O(N)
space, no mutation, omission or shared state.

B<Example>

    const auto h=dm::stats::kruskal_wallis_test({1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9},{3,3,3});
    std::print("{:.1f}\n",h.statistic); // 7.2

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/kruskal.test.html

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$values> (array reference of number)

Finite observations concatenated by group.

=item C<$group_sizes> (array reference of integer)

At least two positive group sizes summing to values.size().

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: statistic=H, parameter1=k-1 and upper-tail approximate p_value; no CI.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Invalid groups, nonfinite input or all observations tied.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub kruskal_wallis_test {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(200, [@_]);
}

=head2 dunn_test

    my $result = dunn_test($values, $group_sizes, $adjustment);

Performs Dunn pairwise comparisons on ranks pooled across all groups.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::dunn_test>.

Tied observations get average ranks, and their reduced pooled rank variance
enters the normal approximation. The standard interpretation assumes
independent groups. A location interpretation uses a common-shape shift model.

B<Example>

    const auto rows = dm::stats::dunn_test({1, 2, 4, 5, 7, 8}, {2, 2, 2});
    std::print("{}\n", rows.size()); // 3

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

Dunn (1964), Multiple Comparisons Using Rank Sums, doi:10.2307/1266041.

=item *

https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/dunn.test/index.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$adjustment = Datamunge::PAdjustMethod::HOLM>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$values> (array reference of number)

Finite observations, concatenated by group.

=item C<$group_sizes> (array reference of integer)

At least two positive counts that partition values.

=item C<$adjustment> (Datamunge::PAdjustMethod constant, optional)

Multiplicity method for the whole pairwise family, Holm by default.

=back

Returns array reference of Datamunge::DunnComparison: Owned comparisons in order (1,0), (2,0), (2,1), and so on.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Invalid groups or zero pooled rank variation.

See also: C<kruskal_wallis_test()>, C<p_adjust()>.

=cut

sub dunn_test {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(201, [@_]);
}

=head2 chi_squared_goodness_of_fit

    my $result = chi_squared_goodness_of_fit($observed, $expected_probabilities);

Pearson chi-square test of specified category probabilities, like R chisq.test().

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::chi_squared_goodness_of_fit>.

Uses sum((observed-expected)^2/expected) and chi-square(k-1); the probabilities are fixed, not
estimated here. Expected counts must be sufficiently large for the approximation. Nonnegative
fractional counts are accepted algebraically but do not automatically define a sampling model.
O(k) work/storage, inputs unchanged, no shared state or omission.

B<Example>

    const auto q=dm::stats::chi_squared_goodness_of_fit({10,10});
    std::print("{:.0f}\n",q.statistic); // 0

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/chisq.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$expected_probabilities = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$observed> (array reference of number)

At least two finite nonnegative counts with positive finite total.

=item C<$expected_probabilities> (array reference of number, optional)

Empty (default) means uniform; otherwise positive finite probabilities matching counts and summing to one within 1e-10.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: statistic=X-squared, parameter1=k-1 and approximate upper-tail p_value; no CI.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Invalid counts/probabilities, sizes or zero/nonfinite total.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub chi_squared_goodness_of_fit {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(202, [@_]);
}

=head2 power_divergence_test

    my $result = power_divergence_test($observed, $expected_probabilities, $power, $estimated_parameters);

Cressie–Read power-divergence goodness-of-fit test for specified probabilities.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::power_divergence_test>.

Power 1 gives Pearson's statistic, 0 the likelihood-ratio G statistic,
and -1 its reverse. A zero observed count is treated by continuity and
yields an infinite divergence when power is at most -1.
Uses an asymptotic chi-square reference.

B<Example>

    const auto fit = dm::stats::power_divergence_test({25, 15}, {.5, .5}, 1);
    std::print("{:.1f}\n", fit.statistic); // 2.5

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.stats.power_divergence.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$expected_probabilities = C++ default>, C<$power = 0.0>, C<$estimated_parameters = 0>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$observed> (array reference of number)

At least two finite nonnegative counts with positive finite total.

=item C<$expected_probabilities> (array reference of number, optional)

Positive finite probabilities summing to one, or empty for uniform.

=item C<$power> (number, optional)

Finite divergence power, default 0.

=item C<$estimated_parameters> (integer, optional)

Degrees of freedom to subtract from k-1, default zero.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: Divergence statistic, degrees of freedom and upper-tail p-value.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Invalid counts, probabilities, power or degrees of freedom.

See also: C<chi_squared_goodness_of_fit()>.

=cut

sub power_divergence_test {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(203, [@_]);
}

=head2 chi_squared_test_independence

    my $result = chi_squared_test_independence($table, $nrows, $ncols, $correct);

Pearson chi-square test for a contingency table, like R chisq.test().

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::chi_squared_test_independence>.

Expected counts are row_sum*column_sum/total; df=(rows-1)(columns-1). The asymptotic reference
assumes independent multinomial/product-multinomial observations and adequately populated
expected cells. O(rows*columns) work and O(rows+columns) space; inputs unchanged, no shared
state.

B<Example>

    const auto q=dm::stats::chi_squared_test_independence({10,10,10,10},2,2);
    std::print("{:.0f}\n",q.statistic); // 0

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/chisq.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$correct = true>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$table> (array reference of number)

Finite nonnegative row-major counts with strictly positive margins; integer counts are the usual sampling model.

=item C<$nrows> (integer)

At least two rows.

=item C<$ncols> (integer)

At least two columns; table length must equal nrows*ncols.

=item C<$correct> (boolean, optional)

True (default) subtracts min(.5,abs(O-E)) from each absolute residual only for 2x2 tables.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: statistic=X-squared, parameter1=df, approximate upper-tail p_value; no CI.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Invalid shape, counts/margins or nonfinite total.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub chi_squared_test_independence {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(204, [@_]);
}

=head2 pearson_correlation_test

    my $result = pearson_correlation_test($x, $y, $alternative, $conf_level);

Pearson product-moment correlation test with Fisher-z confidence bounds.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::pearson_correlation_test>.

Uses t=r*sqrt((n-2)/(1-r^2)). The exact zero-correlation null reference requires independent
bivariate-normal pairs; Fisher-z intervals are approximate. At n=3 no confidence interval is
reported; nE<gt>=4 gets one. Perfect correlations use infinite-statistic limits. O(n) work, no
input mutation/omission or shared state.

B<Example>

    const auto r=dm::stats::pearson_correlation_test({1,2,3,4},{2,4,6,8});
    std::print("{:.1f}\n",r.estimate1); // 1.0

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/cor.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$alternative = Datamunge::Alternative::TWO_SIDED>, C<$conf_level = 0.95>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$x> (array reference of number)

At least three finite nonconstant first measurements.

=item C<$y> (array reference of number)

Same-length finite nonconstant second measurements, paired by index.

=item C<$alternative> (Datamunge::Alternative constant, optional)

TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for population correlation.

=item C<$conf_level> (number, optional)

Finite confidence level in (0,1), default .95.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: statistic=t, parameter1=n-2, estimate1=r and p_value; has_conf_int only for nE<gt>=4.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Invalid lengths, nonfinite/constant samples or invalid controls.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub pearson_correlation_test {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(205, [@_]);
}

=head2 spearman_correlation_test

    my $result = spearman_correlation_test($x, $y, $alternative);

Spearman correlation with average ranks and a t-approximation p-value.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::spearman_correlation_test>.

Computes Pearson correlation on ranks. The reference is always t(n-2), not a small-sample
exact permutation distribution; ties receive average ranks. Tests zero rank correlation under
the approximation, not arbitrary independence. O(n log n) work/O(n) storage, no
mutation/omission or shared state.

B<Example>

    const auto r=dm::stats::spearman_correlation_test({1,2,3,4},{10,20,30,40});
    std::print("{:.1f}\n",r.estimate1); // 1.0

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/cor.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$alternative = Datamunge::Alternative::TWO_SIDED>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$x> (array reference of number)

At least three finite nonconstant first measurements.

=item C<$y> (array reference of number)

Same-length finite nonconstant paired second measurements.

=item C<$alternative> (Datamunge::Alternative constant, optional)

TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for population rank correlation.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: statistic=rank-correlation t, parameter1=n-2, estimate1=rho, approximate p_value; no CI.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Invalid lengths, nonfinite/constant samples or alternative.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub spearman_correlation_test {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(206, [@_]);
}

=head2 kendall_tau

    my $result = kendall_tau($x, $y);

Kendall rank coefficients by Knight inversion counting.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::kendall_tau>.

Sorts paired values and counts concordant, discordant and tied pairs in O(n log n) work/O(n)
storage. Inputs are unchanged and calls share no mutable state. This is a coefficient
calculation, not a hypothesis test; tau_b is zero when its denominator vanishes.

B<Example>

    const auto k=dm::stats::kendall_tau({1,2,3,4},{1,2,3,4});
    std::print("{}\n",k.concordant); // 6

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/cor.html

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$x> (array reference of number)

At least two finite first coordinates.

=item C<$y> (array reference of number)

Equal-length finite second coordinates, paired by index.

=back

Returns Datamunge::KendallTau: Owned pair counts, tau_a and tie-adjusted tau_b.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Lengths differ, fewer than two values, or nonfinite observations.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub kendall_tau {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(207, [@_]);
}

=head2 fisher_exact_test_2x2

    my $result = fisher_exact_test_2x2($a, $b, $c, $d, $alternative);

Conditional Fisher exact test of a 2x2 table with fixed margins.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::fisher_exact_test_2x2>.

Conditions on row/column totals and tests odds ratio one. Two-sided p-values sum probabilities
no larger than the observed table (relative ordering tolerance 1e-7); they are not generally
twice the smaller tail. Direct tail summation avoids 1-CDF cancellation. Empty margins give
p=1. O(s) work/O(1) auxiliary storage for feasible support s; inputs unchanged, no shared
state.

B<Example>

    const auto f=dm::stats::fisher_exact_test_2x2(0,3,3,0);
    std::print("{:.1f}\n",f.p_value); // 0.1

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/fisher.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$alternative = Datamunge::Alternative::TWO_SIDED>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$a> (integer)

Top-left nonnegative integer count.

=item C<$b> (integer)

Top-right count.

=item C<$c> (integer)

Bottom-left count.

=item C<$d> (integer)

Bottom-right count; total must fit size_t.

=item C<$alternative> (Datamunge::Alternative constant, optional)

TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for the odds ratio relative to one.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: statistic=a, p_value and estimate1=sample odds ratio a*d/(b*c), not R conditional MLE; denominator zero gives infinity for positive numerator or NaN for 0/0. No confidence interval.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Count-total overflow or invalid alternative.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub fisher_exact_test_2x2 {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(208, [@_]);
}

=head2 ks_test_one_sample

    my $result = ks_test_one_sample($sample, $cdf, $alternative);

One-sample Kolmogorov–Smirnov test against a supplied continuous CDF.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::ks_test_one_sample>.

Uses the same asymptotic reference as the normal and uniform convenience tests.
The CDF must be fully specified independently of the observations. Values are
checked for range and monotonicity only at the sorted sample points.

B<Example>

    const auto d = dm::stats::ks_test_one_sample({0.2, 0.6}, [](double x) { return x; });

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/ks.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$alternative = Datamunge::Alternative::TWO_SIDED>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$sample> (array reference of number)

At least two finite observations.

=item C<$cdf> (code reference (number) returning number)

Nonempty continuous-null CDF callable, returning values in [0,1].

=item C<$alternative> (Datamunge::Alternative constant, optional)

TwoSided (default), Less if the empirical CDF is below the reference, or Greater if above.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: KS discrepancy, sample size in parameter1, and approximate p-value. Exceptions from the CDF propagate.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If inputs violate the stated constraints.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::bad_alloc>): If allocation fails.

See also: C<ks_test_one_sample_normal()>, C<ks_test_two_sample()>.

=cut

sub ks_test_one_sample {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(209, [@_]);
}

=head2 ks_test_one_sample_normal

    my $result = ks_test_one_sample_normal($x, $mean, $sd, $alternative);

One-sample KS test against a fully specified continuous normal CDF.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::ks_test_one_sample_normal>.

Two-sided p-values use the Stephens corrected asymptotic Kolmogorov reference; one-sided
values use exp(-2*n*D^2). Mean and sd must be fixed independently of this sample: fitting them
here invalidates the stated calibration. This is not a Lilliefors test. Ties are accepted, but
continuous-null calibration is affected by rounding. O(n log n) work/O(n) storage, input
unchanged, no omission/shared state.

B<Example>

    const auto k=dm::stats::ks_test_one_sample_normal({-1,0,1});
    std::print("{:.0f}\n",k.parameter1); // 3

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/ks.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$mean = 0.0>, C<$sd = 1.0>, C<$alternative = Datamunge::Alternative::TWO_SIDED>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$x> (array reference of number)

At least two finite observations.

=item C<$mean> (number, optional)

Finite prespecified normal mean, default zero.

=item C<$sd> (number, optional)

Positive finite prespecified normal SD, default one.

=item C<$alternative> (Datamunge::Alternative constant, optional)

TwoSided (default); Greater tests empirical CDF above reference, Less below (opposite to a location alternative).

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: statistic=D or D+/D-, parameter1=n and approximate p_value; no CI.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Too few/nonfinite values, invalid mean/sd or alternative.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub ks_test_one_sample_normal {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(210, [@_]);
}

=head2 ks_test_one_sample_uniform

    my $result = ks_test_one_sample_uniform($x, $lower, $upper, $alternative);

One-sample KS test against a specified continuous uniform CDF.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::ks_test_one_sample_uniform>.

Same asymptotic p-value and tie caveats as ks_test_one_sample_normal(); limits must be
prespecified rather than estimated from x. O(n log n) work/O(n) storage; no mutation, omission
or shared state.

B<Example>

    const auto k=dm::stats::ks_test_one_sample_uniform({.2,.4,.6,.8});
    std::print("{:.1f}\n",k.statistic); // 0.2

