=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/ks.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$lower = 0.0>, C<$upper = 1.0>, C<$alternative = Datamunge::Alternative::TWO_SIDED>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$x> (array reference of number)

At least two finite observations.

=item C<$lower> (number, optional)

Finite prespecified lower bound, default zero.

=item C<$upper> (number, optional)

Finite upper bound greater than lower, default one.

=item C<$alternative> (Datamunge::Alternative constant, optional)

TwoSided (default), Greater for empirical CDF above reference or Less below.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: statistic=D or D+/D-, parameter1=n, approximate p_value; no CI.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Invalid sample, limits or alternative.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub ks_test_one_sample_uniform {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(211, [@_]);
}

=head2 ks_test_two_sample

    my $result = ks_test_two_sample($x, $y, $alternative);

Two-sample KS comparison of empirical CDFs with asymptotic p-values.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::ks_test_two_sample>.

Uses effective n=n_x*n_y/(n_x+n_y) in the asymptotic reference. This is not an exact finite-
sample calculation; ties/rounding and very small groups affect calibration. O(N log N)
work/O(N) storage; inputs unchanged, no omission/shared state.

B<Example>

    const auto k=dm::stats::ks_test_two_sample({1,2,3},{1,2,3});
    std::print("{:.0f}\n",k.statistic); // 0

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/ks.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$alternative = Datamunge::Alternative::TWO_SIDED>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$x> (array reference of number)

Nonempty finite first sample.

=item C<$y> (array reference of number)

Nonempty finite independent second sample.

=item C<$alternative> (Datamunge::Alternative constant, optional)

TwoSided (default); Greater tests F_x above F_y, Less below, so Greater corresponds to stochastically smaller x.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: statistic=D or D+/D-, parameter1=n_x, parameter2=n_y and approximate p_value; no CI.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Empty/nonfinite samples or invalid alternative.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub ks_test_two_sample {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(212, [@_]);
}

=head2 shapiro_francia_test

    my $result = shapiro_francia_test($x);

Shapiro-Francia composite normality test, like R nortest::sf.test().

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::shapiro_francia_test>.

Squared correlation of sorted observations with Blom normal scores, using Royston (1993) log-
normal p-value approximation. Tests some normal distribution; a large p-value does not prove
normality. Rounding/ties affect calibration. O(n log n) work/O(n) storage; input unchanged, no
omission/shared state.

B<Example>

    const auto s=dm::stats::shapiro_francia_test({1,2,3,4,5});
    std::print("{}\n",s.has_conf_int); // false

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://search.r-project.org/CRAN/refmans/nortest/html/sf.test.html

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$x> (array reference of number)

Between 5 and 5000 finite, nonconstant observations.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: statistic=estimate1=W-prime and approximate p_value; no CI.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Invalid sample size, nonfinite input or constant sample.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub shapiro_francia_test {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(213, [@_]);
}

=head2 proportion_test_one_sample

    my $result = proportion_test_one_sample($successes, $n, $p, $alternative, $correct, $conf_level);

One-proportion score test with an uncorrected Wilson confidence interval.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::proportion_test_one_sample>.

Tests a fixed Bernoulli success probability using the signed null-standard-error z statistic;
statistic stores z^2 but the chosen tail uses its sign. The optional continuity correction
affects the test only: its Wilson interval is uncorrected and need not invert that test.
Approximation requires sufficiently populated success/failure counts. O(1) work/storage, no
shared state.

B<Example>

    const auto p=dm::stats::proportion_test_one_sample(45,100);
    std::print("{:.2f}\n",p.estimate1); // 0.45

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/prop.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$p = 0.5>, C<$alternative = Datamunge::Alternative::TWO_SIDED>, C<$correct = true>, C<$conf_level = 0.95>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$successes> (integer)

Integer successes in [0,n].

=item C<$n> (integer)

Positive independent-trial count.

=item C<$p> (number, optional)

Finite null probability strictly in (0,1), default .5.

=item C<$alternative> (Datamunge::Alternative constant, optional)

TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for true probability relative to p.

=item C<$correct> (boolean, optional)

True (default) applies the capped half-count correction to z.

=item C<$conf_level> (number, optional)

Finite confidence level in (0,1), default .95.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: statistic=z^2, parameter1=1, estimate1=successes/n, p_value and uncorrected Wilson confidence bounds.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Invalid counts, null probability, alternative or confidence level.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub proportion_test_one_sample {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(214, [@_]);
}

=head2 proportion_test_two_sample

    my $result = proportion_test_two_sample($successes1, $n1, $successes2, $n2, $alternative, $correct, $conf_level);

Pooled score test for two proportions, with an unpooled difference interval.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::proportion_test_two_sample>.

Tests equality using the pooled null SE and optionally a capped continuity correction. The
difference CI uses an unpooled Wald SE and an uncapped half-count width correction when
requested; it is approximate and is not a Wilson/Newcombe interval. All-zero/all-one pooled
samples return z=0,p=1. O(1) work/storage, no shared state.

B<Example>

    const auto p=dm::stats::proportion_test_two_sample(2,10,3,10);
    std::print("{:.1f}\n",p.estimate1); // 0.2

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/prop.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$alternative = Datamunge::Alternative::TWO_SIDED>, C<$correct = true>, C<$conf_level = 0.95>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$successes1> (integer)

First success count in [0,n1].

=item C<$n1> (integer)

Positive first independent-trial count.

=item C<$successes2> (integer)

Second success count in [0,n2].

=item C<$n2> (integer)

Positive independent second-trial count.

=item C<$alternative> (Datamunge::Alternative constant, optional)

TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for p1-p2.

=item C<$correct> (boolean, optional)

True (default) corrects the score test and adds a half-count CI width.

=item C<$conf_level> (number, optional)

Finite confidence level in (0,1), default .95.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: statistic=z^2, parameter1=1, estimates=sample proportions, p_value and confidence bounds for p1-p2, clamped to [-1,1].

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Invalid counts, alternative or confidence level.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub proportion_test_two_sample {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(215, [@_]);
}

=head2 binomial_test

    my $result = binomial_test($successes, $n, $p, $alternative, $conf_level);

Exact binomial test with Clopper-Pearson confidence bounds, like R binom.test().

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::binomial_test>.

One-sided p-values are inclusive tails. Two-sided p-values sum PMFs no larger than the
observed PMF, using relative comparison tolerance 1e-7. The equal-tailed Clopper-Pearson
interval has at-least-nominal coverage but need not invert the probability-ordered two-sided
test. Null endpoints zero/one are supported. Two-sided enumeration costs O(n), intervals use
bounded beta-CDF bisection; O(1) auxiliary storage, no shared state.

B<Example>

    const auto b=dm::stats::binomial_test(9,10);
    std::print("{:.6f}\n",b.p_value); // 0.021484

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/binom.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$p = 0.5>, C<$alternative = Datamunge::Alternative::TWO_SIDED>, C<$conf_level = 0.95>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$successes> (integer)

Integer observed successes in [0,n].

=item C<$n> (integer)

Positive independent Bernoulli trial count.

=item C<$p> (number, optional)

Finite null probability in [0,1], default .5.

=item C<$alternative> (Datamunge::Alternative constant, optional)

TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for true success probability.

=item C<$conf_level> (number, optional)

Finite confidence level in (0,1), default .95.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: statistic=successes, parameter1=n, estimate1=successes/n, p_value and Clopper-Pearson interval; one-sided intervals end at zero/one.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Invalid counts, probability, alternative or confidence level.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub binomial_test {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(216, [@_]);
}

=head2 newcombe_difference_interval

    my $result = newcombe_difference_interval($successes1, $n1, $successes2, $n2, $confidence_level);

Combines separate Wilson score intervals to estimate a difference in proportions.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::newcombe_difference_interval>.

Implements Newcombe's uncorrected method 10 for two independent binomial samples.

B<Example>

    const auto ci = dm::stats::newcombe_difference_interval(0, 10, 0, 10);
    std::print("{:.4f} {:.4f}\n", ci.lower, ci.upper); // -0.2775 0.2775

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

Newcombe (1998), Interval estimation for the difference between independent proportions,
https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9595617/.

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$confidence_level = 0.95>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$successes1> (integer)

Successes in the first sample, from zero to n1.

=item C<$n1> (integer)

Positive size of the first sample.

=item C<$successes2> (integer)

Successes in the second sample, from zero to n2.

=item C<$n2> (integer)

Positive size of the second sample.

=item C<$confidence_level> (number, optional)

Two-sided nominal confidence level in (0,1), default .95.

=back

Returns Datamunge::ProportionDifferenceInterval: Difference and bounds on the probability scale.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Invalid counts or confidence level.

See also: C<proportion_test_two_sample()>.

=cut

sub newcombe_difference_interval {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(217, [@_]);
}

=head2 risk_ratio_score_interval

    my $result = risk_ratio_score_interval($successes1, $n1, $successes2, $n2, $confidence_level);

Inverts the uncorrected Koopman two-binomial score test for a risk ratio.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::risk_ratio_score_interval>.

At each candidate ratio, the binomial likelihood is maximized subject
to that ratio. The fitted risks determine the score variance. This is a
two-sided, asymptotic interval. The estimate is positive infinity when
only the denominator risk is zero. When both counts are zero, the
estimate is absent and the interval is [0, +infinity].

B<Example>

    const auto ci = dm::stats::risk_ratio_score_interval(0, 20, 4, 20);
    std::print("{} {:.3f}\n", ci.lower, ci.upper); // 0 0.870

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

P. A. R. Koopman (1984), https://doi.org/10.2307/2531405

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$confidence_level = 0.95>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$successes1> (integer)

Successes in the first sample, from zero to n1.

=item C<$n1> (integer)

Positive first sample size.

=item C<$successes2> (integer)

Successes in the second sample, from zero to n2.

=item C<$n2> (integer)

Positive second sample size.

=item C<$confidence_level> (number, optional)

Two-sided nominal confidence level in (0,1), default .95.

=back

Returns Datamunge::RiskRatioInterval: Risk ratio estimate and lower and upper score bounds.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Invalid counts or confidence level.

See also: C<newcombe_difference_interval()>, L<Datamunge::RiskRatioInterval|/Datamunge::RiskRatioInterval>.

=cut

sub risk_ratio_score_interval {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(218, [@_]);
}

=head2 poisson_rate_interval

    my $result = poisson_rate_interval($events, $exposure, $confidence_level);

Inverts Poisson count tails for a Garwood interval on one event rate.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::poisson_rate_interval>.

B<Example>

    const auto ci = dm::stats::poisson_rate_interval(0, 5);
    std::print("{:.3f}\n", ci.upper); // 0.738

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/poisson.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$confidence_level = 0.95>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$events> (integer)

Number of observed events, including zero.

=item C<$exposure> (number)

Positive finite exposure in caller-selected units.

=item C<$confidence_level> (number, optional)

Two-sided nominal coverage in (0,1), default .95.

=back

Returns Datamunge::PoissonRateInterval: Observed rate and interval in events per unit of exposure.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Invalid exposure or confidence level.

