=head2 cochran_armitage_test

    my $result = cochran_armitage_test($successes, $trials, $scores, $alternative);

Cochran–Armitage score test for a linear trend in independent binomial proportions.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::cochran_armitage_test>.

A positive statistic indicates that success is associated with larger
category scores. The standard normal null reference is asymptotic.

B<Example>

    const auto fit = dm::stats::cochran_armitage_test({2, 5, 8}, {10, 10, 10});
    std::print("{:.3f}\n", fit.statistic); // 2.683

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/prop.trend.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$scores = C++ default>, C<$alternative = Datamunge::Alternative::TWO_SIDED>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$successes> (array reference of integer)

One success count per category, at most the corresponding trial count.

=item C<$trials> (array reference of integer)

Positive trial counts for at least two categories.

=item C<$scores> (array reference of number, optional)

Finite numeric category scores, or empty to use 1, 2, ... in input order.

=item C<$alternative> (Datamunge::Alternative constant, optional)

Tail of the signed statistic, two-sided by default.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: Signed z statistic and approximate p-value.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Invalid input, constant scores, or a constant outcome.

See also: C<proportion_test_two_sample()>.

=cut

