    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(221, [@_]);
}

=head2 t_test_one_sample

    my $result = t_test_one_sample($x, $mu, $alternative, $conf_level);

One-sample Student t-test of a population mean, like R t.test().

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::t_test_one_sample>.

Independent normal observations give t=(mean-mu)/SE with n-1 df. The CI estimates the
population mean, not mean-mu. Inputs are copied only for paired differences; all routines
leave inputs unchanged and use no shared mutable state. O(n) work; no null/nonfinite omission.

B<Example>

    const auto t=dm::stats::t_test_one_sample({1,2,3,4});
    std::print("{:.0f}\n",t.parameter1); // 3

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/t.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$mu = 0.0>, C<$alternative = Datamunge::Alternative::TWO_SIDED>, C<$conf_level = 0.95>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$x> (array reference of number)

At least two finite observations with positive representable sample variance.

=item C<$mu> (number, optional)

Finite null mean, default zero, in observation units.

=item C<$alternative> (Datamunge::Alternative constant, optional)

TwoSided (default), Less or Greater, relative to mu.

=item C<$conf_level> (number, optional)

Finite confidence level strictly in (0,1), default .95.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: statistic=t, parameter1=df, estimate1=sample mean, p_value and mean confidence bounds; remaining scalars retain defaults. One-sided intervals have one infinite endpoint.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Too few/nonfinite values, invalid mu/alternative/confidence or zero/nonfinite standard error.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub t_test_one_sample {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(222, [@_]);
}

=head2 t_test_two_sample

    my $result = t_test_two_sample($x, $y, $equal_variance, $alternative, $conf_level);

Independent two-sample Welch or pooled Student mean test, like R t.test().

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::t_test_two_sample>.

Welch uses unequal sample variances and Satterthwaite approximate df; normality alone does not
make its reference exact. Pooled Student requires equal population variances and independent
normal observations for its exact reference. CI estimates mean(x)-mean(y). O(n_x+n_y) work,
inputs unchanged, no shared mutable state or omission.

B<Example>

    const auto t=dm::stats::t_test_two_sample({1,2,3},{4,5,6});
    std::print("{:.0f}\n",t.estimate1); // 2

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/t.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$equal_variance = false>, C<$alternative = Datamunge::Alternative::TWO_SIDED>, C<$conf_level = 0.95>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$x> (array reference of number)

At least two finite observations in the first group.

=item C<$y> (array reference of number)

At least two finite independent observations in the second group.

=item C<$equal_variance> (boolean, optional)

False (default) uses Welch; true uses pooled variance.

=item C<$alternative> (Datamunge::Alternative constant, optional)

TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for mean(x)-mean(y).

=item C<$conf_level> (number, optional)

Finite confidence level in (0,1), default .95.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: statistic=t, parameter1=df, estimate1/2=group means, p_value and confidence bounds for their difference. One-sided bounds can be infinite.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Invalid sample sizes/values, alternative/confidence, unrepresentable mean difference or zero/nonfinite combined standard error.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub t_test_two_sample {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(223, [@_]);
}

=head2 t_test_paired

    my $result = t_test_paired($x, $y, $alternative, $conf_level);

Paired Student t-test on aligned differences, like R t.test(paired=TRUE).

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::t_test_paired>.

Runs the one-sample test on x[i]-y[i]. Differences, rather than the two marginal samples, need
independent normal sampling for the exact t reference. Row order defines pairing; no values
are omitted. O(n) work/storage; inputs unchanged, no shared mutable state.

B<Example>

    const auto t=dm::stats::t_test_paired({2,4,7},{1,2,4});
    std::print("{:.0f}\n",t.parameter1); // 2

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/t.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$alternative = Datamunge::Alternative::TWO_SIDED>, C<$conf_level = 0.95>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$x> (array reference of number)

At least two finite first measurements.

=item C<$y> (array reference of number)

Equal-length finite second measurements paired by index.

=item C<$alternative> (Datamunge::Alternative constant, optional)

TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for the population mean difference.

=item C<$conf_level> (number, optional)

Finite confidence level in (0,1), default .95.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: One-sample difference result with method Paired t-test; estimate1 is mean difference, CI targets the same difference.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Length mismatch, nonfinite data/differences, invalid controls or zero/nonfinite difference standard error.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub t_test_paired {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(224, [@_]);
}

=head2 f_test_variance

    my $result = f_test_variance($x, $y, $alternative, $conf_level);

F-test and interval for a ratio of variances, like R var.test().

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::f_test_variance>.

Uses sample variance(x)/sample variance(y). Independent normal populations give the
F(df_x,df_y) reference; it is sensitive to nonnormality and is not a gatekeeper for choosing
Welch versus pooled t tests. O(n_x+n_y) work/O(1) auxiliary storage; inputs unchanged, no
omission/shared state.

B<Example>

    const auto f=dm::stats::f_test_variance({1,2,3},{2,4,6});
    std::print("{:.2f}\n",f.estimate1); // 0.25

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/var.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$alternative = Datamunge::Alternative::TWO_SIDED>, C<$conf_level = 0.95>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$x> (array reference of number)

At least two finite first-sample values with positive finite sample variance.

=item C<$y> (array reference of number)

At least two finite independent second-sample values with positive finite sample variance.

=item C<$alternative> (Datamunge::Alternative constant, optional)

TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for population variance ratio relative to one.

=item C<$conf_level> (number, optional)

Finite confidence level in (0,1), default .95.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: statistic=estimate1=variance ratio, parameter1/2=sample sizes minus one, p_value and ratio confidence bounds; one-sided interval ends at zero or infinity.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Too few/nonfinite observations, nonpositive/nonfinite variances or invalid controls.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub f_test_variance {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(225, [@_]);
}

=head2 wilcoxon_signed_rank_test

    my $result = wilcoxon_signed_rank_test($x, $mu, $alternative);

Normal-approximation Wilcoxon signed-rank test with tie/continuity corrections.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::wilcoxon_signed_rank_test>.

Tests symmetry of x-mu about zero under independent observations, not an arbitrary-population
mean or median null. For paired data pass aligned differences. Exact-zero differences are
removed; absolute differences receive average ranks. Always uses a normal approximation, even
for small tie-free samples; no exact option or CI. O(n log n) work/O(n) storage; input
unchanged, no shared state.

B<Example>

    const auto w=dm::stats::wilcoxon_signed_rank_test({1,2,3,4});
    std::print("{:.0f}\n",w.statistic); // 10

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/wilcox.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$mu = 0.0>, C<$alternative = Datamunge::Alternative::TWO_SIDED>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$x> (array reference of number)

Finite values; at least one nonzero difference from mu must remain.

=item C<$mu> (number, optional)

Finite symmetry centre, default zero.

=item C<$alternative> (Datamunge::Alternative constant, optional)

TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for the signed location shift.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: statistic=positive rank sum V, estimate1=mu (not an estimated pseudomedian), approximate p_value; no CI.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Nonfinite input/mu, invalid alternative, no nonzero differences or zero rank variance.

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub wilcoxon_signed_rank_test {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(226, [@_]);
}

=head2 wilcoxon_rank_sum_test

    my $result = wilcoxon_rank_sum_test($x, $y, $alternative);

Tie/continuity-corrected Mann-Whitney U test using a normal approximation.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::stats::wilcoxon_rank_sum_test>.

Pools ranks, giving average ranks to ties, and reports U for the first sample. Its identical-
distribution null requires independent exchangeable samples. A common-shape shift model
supports a location interpretation; equal medians alone is insufficient. U/(n_x*n_y) estimates
P(XE<gt>Y)+P(X=Y)/2, not a mean difference. Approximation is used at every size. O(N log N)
work/O(N) storage, inputs unchanged, no shared state.

B<Example>

    const auto w=dm::stats::wilcoxon_rank_sum_test({1,2,3},{4,5,6});
    std::print("{:.0f}\n",w.statistic); // 0

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/wilcox.test.html

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$alternative = Datamunge::Alternative::TWO_SIDED>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$x> (array reference of number)

Nonempty finite first sample.

=item C<$y> (array reference of number)

Nonempty finite independent second sample.

=item C<$alternative> (Datamunge::Alternative constant, optional)

TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for first-versus-second stochastic location.

=back

Returns Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult: statistic=U=rank_sum(x)-n_x(n_x+1)/2 and approximate p_value; no CI or location estimate.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): Empty/nonfinite samples, invalid alternative or zero rank variance (all pooled observations tied).

See also: L<Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult|/Datamunge::HypothesisTestResult>.

=cut

sub wilcoxon_rank_sum_test {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(227, [@_]);
}

=head2 bs

    my $result = bs($lower, $upper, $knots, $degree, $intercept);

Construct an owned B-spline basis for repeated evaluation with eval().
Free-function spelling of Bspline: construction fixes the knots and boundaries
without evaluating a design matrix or fitting a model. The returned value owns
its basis; the input knot vector may be modified or destroyed after this call.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::bs>.

B<Example>

    const auto basis = dm::bs(0, 1, dm::seql(0, 1, 3));
    const auto design = basis.eval(dm::c(0, 0.5, 1));
    std::print("{} {}\n", design.rows(), design.cols()); // 3 4

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$degree = 3>, C<$intercept = false>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$lower> (number)

Finite left boundary, strictly below upper.

=item C<$upper> (number)

Finite right boundary.

=item C<$knots> (array reference of number)

Finite, strictly increasing breakpoints in [lower, upper]. Boundary entries are optional; an empty vector creates a basis without interior knots.

=item C<$degree> (integer, optional)

Polynomial degree, default 3 (cubic).

=item C<$intercept> (boolean, optional)

Keep the first basis column, default false. Leave false with an intercept formula such as C<y ~ .>; use a no-intercept formula when true.

=back

Returns Datamunge::Bspline: Owned Bspline with the same degree, intercept policy and validation as its constructor. Call eval() on training and prediction coordinates.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): For invalid boundaries, knots or overflowing degree.

See also: L<Datamunge::Bspline|/Datamunge::Bspline>, C<Bspline::eval()>.

=cut

sub bs {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(228, [@_]);
}

=head2 mkdir_

    my $result = mkdir_($path);

Create a directory and any missing parent directories.
Accepts native filesystem paths, strings and string literals. An existing
directory is a successful no-op; an existing file at the target or along its
parents is an error. Relative paths use the current working directory, which
this function does not change. Permissions follow the native filesystem and
process defaults. A failed recursive operation may leave created parents.
This C++ convenience forwards to std::filesystem::create_directories().

Calls C++ C<datamunge::mkdir>.

B<Example>

    dm::mkdir("build/figs");
    std::print("{}\n", std::filesystem::is_directory("build/figs")); // true

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$path> (string)

