=head2 refine_basis

    my $result = refine_basis($basis, $inserted_knots);

Inserts knots into a clamped B-spline basis (Oslo algorithm).

Calls C++ C<datamunge::fda::refine_basis>.

Builds the refined knot vector (old knots merged with C<inserted_knots>) and the sparse
discrete-B-spline operator that re-expresses every coarse spline on it exactly. Each
row has at most degree + 1 nonzeros and the work is O(m p^2) for m refined functions.

B<Example>

    auto coarse = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(1, 2);   // knots 0 0 1 1
    auto r = dm::fda::refine_basis(coarse, {0.25});
    std::print("{}\n", r.apply({0.0, 4.0}));                   // [0, 1, 4]

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

E. Cohen, T. Lyche and R. Riesenfeld (1980). Discrete B-splines and subdivision
techniques in computer-aided geometric design and computer graphics. Computer Graphics
and Image Processing 14(2), 87-111. https://doi.org/10.1016/0146-664X(80)90040-4

=item *

W. Boehm (1980). Inserting new knots into B-spline curves. Computer-Aided Design
12(4), 199-201. https://doi.org/10.1016/0010-4485(80)90154-2

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$basis> (Datamunge::BSplineBasis)

Basis to refine; its knot vector must be clamped (first and last knots repeated degree + 1 times, as produced by BSplineBasis::open_uniform()).

=item C<$inserted_knots> (array reference of number)

Knots to add, strictly inside the domain, in any order. Repeats are allowed while every knot multiplicity stays E<lt>= degree + 1.

=back

Returns Datamunge::BSplineRefinement: The refined basis and its refinement operator.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If the basis is not clamped, a knot is outside the open domain or non-finite, or a multiplicity would exceed degree + 1.

See also: C<dyadic_refine()>.

=cut

sub refine_basis {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(237, [@_]);
}

=head2 dyadic_refine

    my $result = dyadic_refine($basis);

Halves every knot span of a clamped basis (inserts each positive span's midpoint).

Calls C++ C<datamunge::fda::dyadic_refine>.

This is the nested-space step between consecutive levels of HB/THB splines: the refined
space contains the coarse one, and the operator maps coefficients exactly.

B<Example>

    auto r = dm::fda::dyadic_refine(dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(3, 5));
    std::print("{} -> {}\n", 5, r.basis.basis_size()); // 5 -> 7

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

E. Cohen, T. Lyche and R. Riesenfeld (1980). Discrete B-splines and subdivision
techniques in computer-aided geometric design and computer graphics.
https://doi.org/10.1016/0146-664X(80)90040-4

=back

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$basis> (Datamunge::BSplineBasis)

Clamped basis to refine.

=back

Returns Datamunge::BSplineRefinement: The refined basis and its refinement operator.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): If the basis is not clamped.

See also: C<refine_basis()>, C<TensorBSpline::dyadic_refine()>.

=cut

sub dyadic_refine {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(238, [@_]);
}

=head2 fit_tensor_bspline

    my $result = fit_tensor_bspline($bases, $points, $values, $options);

Fits a tensor-product B-spline to scattered data by ordinary least squares.

Calls C++ C<datamunge::fda::fit_tensor_bspline>.

Builds the sparse design B (each row has at most prod(degree_k + 1) nonzeros), then
solves (B^T B) c = B^T y. Coordinates outside a basis domain are clamped to it.

B<Example>

    auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(1, 2);
    auto f = dm::fda::fit_tensor_bspline({b}, {{0.0}, {0.5}, {1.0}}, {1.0, 2.0, 3.0});
    std::print("{:.3f}\n", f.evaluate(0.25)); // 1.500

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

C. de Boor (2001). A Practical Guide to Splines, revised edition. Springer.

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$options = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$bases> (array reference of Datamunge::BSplineBasis)

One basis per dimension.

=item C<$points> (array reference of array reference of number)

Sample locations, each of length bases.size().

=item C<$values> (array reference of number)

One response per point.

=item C<$options> (Datamunge::SplineSolveOptions, optional)

Solver settings (default: automatic).

=back

Returns Datamunge::TensorBSpline: The fitted spline.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): On empty or mismatched inputs, or non-finite data.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::runtime_error>): If the normal equations are singular (some basis function has too little data; use a penalized fit or fewer functions).

See also: C<fit_tensor_pspline()>.

=cut

sub fit_tensor_bspline {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(239, [@_]);
}

=head2 fit_tensor_pspline

    my $result = fit_tensor_pspline($bases, $points, $values, $lambdas, $order, $options);

Fits a tensor-product P-spline: least squares plus an anisotropic difference
penalty, solving (B^T B + P) c = B^T y with P from tensor_difference_penalty().

Calls C++ C<datamunge::fda::fit_tensor_pspline>.

All-zero lambdas reproduce fit_tensor_bspline(). Positive lambdas keep the system
well-posed even with fewer points than coefficients (for order 0, or once the data
determine the order-q polynomial null space).

B<Example>

    auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(3, 8);
    auto f = dm::fda::fit_tensor_pspline({b}, {{0.0}, {1.0}}, {0.0, 1.0}, {1e3});
    std::print("{:.3f}\n", f.evaluate(0.5)); // 0.500

B<References>

=over 4

=item *

P. H. C. Eilers and B. D. Marx (1996). Flexible smoothing with B-splines and penalties.
https://doi.org/10.1214/ss/1038425655

=item *

P. H. C. Eilers, I. D. Currie and M. Durban (2006). Fast and compact smoothing on large
multidimensional grids. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.csda.2004.07.008

=back

Arguments left out take the C++ defaults: C<$order = 2>, C<$options = C++ default>.

Parameters:

=over 4

=item C<$bases> (array reference of Datamunge::BSplineBasis)

One basis per dimension.

=item C<$points> (array reference of array reference of number)

Sample locations, each of length bases.size().

=item C<$values> (array reference of number)

One response per point.

=item C<$lambdas> (array reference of number)

Nonnegative per-axis smoothing weights, one per basis.

=item C<$order> (integer, optional)

Difference order (default 2, the conventional curvature penalty).

=item C<$options> (Datamunge::SplineSolveOptions, optional)

Solver settings (default: automatic).

=back

Returns Datamunge::TensorBSpline: The fitted spline.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::InvalidArgument> (C++ C<std::invalid_argument>): On empty/mismatched inputs, invalid lambdas, or an axis with no more functions than C<order>.

Dies with C<Datamunge::Error::Native> (C++ C<std::runtime_error>): If the penalized normal equations are singular.

See also: C<fit_tensor_bspline()>, C<fit_pspline()>.

=cut

sub fit_tensor_pspline {
    return Datamunge::Rosetta::call(240, [@_]);
}

